#{[17592186618893 :question/problem "![](images/b7/b7c94487c10ac3eb005d22066a8904591415e9b92f520b18bf61031f67e93605.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows train routes between three stations, where nodes represent the stations and arrows represent the directed routes between them. The (incomplete) route matrix below represents information about the number of routes between each of the three stations. Find the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n<div class=\"route-matrix\">\n  <span class=\"matrix-to\">To</span>\n  <span class=\"matrix-from\">From</span>\n  <table aria-label=\"Route matrix: rows are From stations; columns are To stations\">\n    <thead>\n      <tr><td class=\"matrix-corner\"></td><th scope=\"col\">A</th><th scope=\"col\">B</th><th scope=\"col\">C</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">A</th><td>$0$</td><td>$3$</td><td>$3$</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">B</th><td>$c$</td><td>$0$</td><td>$a$</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">C</th><td>$0$</td><td>$b$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>"] [17592186618893 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our diagram.\n\n- There are no arrows starting at Station B and ending at Station C. So, there are no routes from $B$ to $C,$ and so $a=0.$\n\n- There are no arrows starting at Station C and ending at Station B. So, there are no routes from $C$ to $B,$ and so $b=0.$\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station B and ending at Station A. So, there is only one route from $B$ to $A,$ and so $c=1.$\n\nAs a result, our route matrix is the following:\n\n<div class=\"route-matrix\">\n  <span class=\"matrix-to\">To</span>\n  <span class=\"matrix-from\">From</span>\n  <table aria-label=\"Route matrix: rows are From stations; columns are To stations\">\n    <thead>\n      <tr><td class=\"matrix-corner\"></td><th scope=\"col\">A</th><th scope=\"col\">B</th><th scope=\"col\">C</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">A</th><td>$0$</td><td>$3$</td><td>$3$</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">B</th><td>$1$</td><td>$0$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">C</th><td>$0$</td><td>$0$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\na+b+c=0+0+1=1.\n$$"] [17592186619780 :question/problem "A shipping system has a $40\\%$ chance of labeling a package incorrectly. If $5$ packages are labeled, rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that at most $1$ of them is mislabeled?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186619780 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of mislabeled packages.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=5$ packages.\n\n- The probability of mislabeling is $p=40\\%=0.4.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.4).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.4)^{x}(1-0.4)^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.4)^{x}(0.6)^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at most $1$ of the $5$ packages is mislabeled. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0})(0.4)^{0}(0.6)^{5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.4)^{1}(0.6)^{4} \\\\  & =1(0.6)^{5}+5(0.4)(0.6)^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.337,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186625496 :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\nI. The number of students that pass a literature exam among a group of $40$ students if each of the students passes the exam with a probability of $80\\%$\n\nII. The number of participants in an Olympic Games\n\nIII. The number of times $2$ is rolled when rolling a die $6$ times"] [17592186625501 :question/problem "A store marked up the price of a chair to $140\\%$ of its original cost. A service fee of $25\\%$ was added based on the marked-up price. If the chair originally cost $\\$120,$ what is the service fee?\n\nThe service fee is $\\$$  \n{{field-1}}."] [17592186625501 :question/worked-solution "First, the marked-up price is $140\\%$ of the original cost $(\\$120):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}140\\%\\times 120 & =\\frac{140}{100}\\times 120 \\\\  & =1.4\\times 120 \\\\  & =168\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the marked-up price of the chair is $\\$168.$ Then, the service fee is $25\\%$ of this price:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25\\%\\times 168 & =\\frac{25}{100}\\times 168 \\\\  & =0.25\\times 168 \\\\  & =42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the service fee is $\\$42.$"] [17592186625507 :question/problem "After a $35\\%$ decrease, a store’s weekly revenue is $\\$13,000.$ What was the original revenue?\n\nThe original revenue was $\\$${{field-1}}."] [17592186625507 :question/worked-solution "Decreasing a number by $35\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%-35\\%=65\\%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n65\\%=\\frac{65}{100}=0.65.\n$$\n\nLet the original revenue be $x.$ So, after a $35\\%$ decrease, the new value is\n\n$$\n0.65x.\n$$\n\nWe are told the current revenue is $\\$13,000.$ So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n0.65x=13000.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.65x & =13000 \\\\ x & =\\frac{13000}{0.65} \\\\ x & =20000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the original revenue was $\\$\\,20,000.$"] [17592186625517 :question/problem "After decreasing a quantity $x$ by $35\\%,$ the result is $39.$ What is the value of $x?$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186625517 :question/worked-solution "Decreasing a number by $35\\%$ means that we must apply a percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%-35\\%=65\\%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n65\\%=\\frac{65}{100}=0.65.\n$$\n\nWe're told that by decreasing a number $x$ by $35\\%,$ we get the number $39.$ So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n0.65x=39.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{39}{0.65} \\\\  & =60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $x=60.$"] [17592186625521 :question/problem "After decreasing a quantity $x$ by $50\\%,$ the result is $30.$ What is the value of $x?$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186625521 :question/worked-solution "Decreasing a number by $50\\%$ means that we must apply a percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%-50\\%=50\\%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n50\\%=\\frac{50}{100}=0.5.\n$$\n\nWe're told that by decreasing a number $x$ by $50\\%,$ we get the number $30.$ So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n0.5x=30.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{30}{0.5} \\\\  & =60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $x=60.$"] [17592186625546 :question/problem "A student's savings account balance grew to $130\\%$ of its previous total. Of this new balance, $15\\%$ came from earned interest. What percentage of the previous total balance came from earned interest?"] [17592186625546 :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the previous total balance.\n\nWe're told that the total balance grew to $130\\%$ of its previous total. Thus, the current balance is $1.30x.$\n\nWe are also told that of this new balance, $15\\%$ came from earned interest. So, the amount that came from earned interest is\n\n$$\n0.15(1.30x)=0.195x.\n$$\n\nThe expression $0.195x$ represents $19.5\\%$ of $x.$ Therefore, $19.5\\%$ of the previous total balance came from earned interest."] [17592186625551 :question/problem "After decreasing a quantity $x$ by $10\\%,$ the result is $54.$ What is the value of $x?$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186625551 :question/worked-solution "Decreasing a number by $10\\%$ means that we must apply a percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%-10\\%=90\\%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n90\\%=\\frac{90}{100}=0.9.\n$$\n\nWe're told that by decreasing a number $x$ by $10\\%,$ we get the number $54.$ So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n0.9x=54.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{54}{0.9} \\\\  & =60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $x=60.$"] [17592186625558 :question/problem "After decreasing a quantity $x$ by $30\\%,$ the result is $49.$ What is the value of $x?$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186625558 :question/worked-solution "Decreasing a number by $30\\%$ means that we must apply a percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%-30\\%=70\\%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n70\\%=\\frac{70}{100}=0.7.\n$$\n\nWe're told that by decreasing a number $x$ by $30\\%,$ we get the number $49.$ So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n0.7x=49.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{49}{0.7} \\\\  & =70\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $x=70.$"] [17592186625559 :question/problem "After a $20\\%$ population decrease, the number of residents in a town became $8,000.$ What was the original population?\n\nThe original population of the town was\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186625559 :question/worked-solution "Decreasing a number by $20\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%-20\\%=80\\%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n80\\%=\\frac{80}{100}=0.8.\n$$\n\nLet the original population be $x.$ So, after a $20\\%$ decrease, the new value is\n\n$$\n0.8x.\n$$\n\nWe are told the new population is $8,000.$ So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n0.8x=8000.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.8x & =8000 \\\\ x & =\\frac{8000}{0.8} \\\\ x & =10000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the original population was $10,000.$"] [17592186625560 :question/problem "A soccer team has a $70\\%$ chance of winning each game in an $8$-game tournament. What is the probability that the team wins at least $7$ of the $8$ games? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186625560 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of games that the team wins.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ games\n\n- the probability of winning each game is $p=70\\%=0.7$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(8,0.7).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.7)^{x}(1-0.7)^{8-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.7)^{x}(0.3)^{8-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the soccer team wins at least $7$ of the $8$ games. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 7) & =P(X=7)+P(X=8) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{7})(0.7)^{7}(0.3)^{8-7}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{8})(0.7)^{8}(0.3)^{8-8} \\\\  & =8(0.7)^{7}(0.3)+1(0.7)^{8} \\\\  & =0.255\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186625564 :question/problem "In the emergency room of a hospital, $45\\%$ of patients admitted are due to a traffic accident. If $8$ patients are admitted to the emergency room, what is the probability that at most $6$ of the admissions are due to traffic accidents? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186625564 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of patients admitted due to traffic accidents.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ patients\n\n- the probability that each patient is admitted due to a traffic accident is $p=45\\%=0.45$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(8,0.45).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.45)^{x}(1-0.55)^{8-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.45)^{x}(0.55)^{8-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at most $6$ of the patients are due to traffic accidents. This corresponds to $P(X\\le 6).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 6)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=6)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =1-P(X>6) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=7)+P(X=8)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{7})(0.45)^{7}(0.55)^{8-7}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{8})(0.45)^{8}(0.55)^{8-8}] \\\\  & =1-[8(0.45)^{7}(0.55)+(0.45)^{8}] \\\\  & =1-0.018\\,123 \\\\  & =0.982\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186625570 :question/problem "According to historical data, $85\\%$ of moviegoers said they liked watching movies. Of those, $20\\%$ preferred comedies. What percentage of all moviegoers preferred comedies?"] [17592186625570 :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the total number of moviegoers.\n\nWe're told that $85\\%$ of moviegoers liked watching movies. Thus, the number of moviegoers who like movies is $0.85x.$\n\nWe are also told that of those moviegoers, $20\\%$ preferred comedies. So, the number of moviegoers who preferred comedies is\n\n$$\n0.20(0.85x)=0.17x.\n$$\n\nThe expression $0.17x$ represents $17\\%$ of $x.$ Therefore, $17\\%$ of all moviegoers preferred comedies."] [17592186625574 :question/problem "After decreasing a quantity $x$ by $15\\%,$ the result is $68.$ What is the value of $x?$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186625574 :question/worked-solution "Decreasing a number by $15\\%$ means that we must apply a percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%-15\\%=85\\%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n85\\%=\\frac{85}{100}=0.85.\n$$\n\nWe're told that by decreasing a number $x$ by $15\\%,$ we get the number $68.$ So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n0.85x=68.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{68}{0.85} \\\\  & =80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $x=80.$"] [17592186625575 :question/problem "At a fruit stand, some apples were sold. After $15\\%$ of the original apples were sold, $340$ apples remained. How many apples were sold?"] [17592186625575 :question/worked-solution "Decreasing a number by $15\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%-15\\%=85\\%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n85\\%=\\frac{85}{100}=0.85.\n$$\n\nLet the original number of apples be $x.$ So, after a $15\\%$ decrease, the new value is \n\n$$\n0.85x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $340$ apples remained. So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n0.85x=340.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.85x & =340 \\\\ x & =\\frac{340}{0.85} \\\\ x & =400\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the original number of apples was $400,$ we find the number removed by taking $15\\%$ of $400:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{100}\\cdot 400=60.\n$$\n\nSo, $60$ apples were sold."] [17592186625577 :question/problem "After a $10\\%$ salary cut, an employee now earns $\\$900$ per week. What was the original salary?\n\nThe original weekly salary was $\\$${{field-1}}."] [17592186625577 :question/worked-solution "Decreasing a number by $10\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%-10\\%=90\\%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n90\\%=\\frac{90}{100}=0.9.\n$$\n\nLet the original salary be $x.$ So, after a $10\\%$ decrease, the new value is\n\n$$\n0.9x.\n$$\n\nWe are told the new salary is $\\$900.$ So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n0.9x=900.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.9x & =900 \\\\ x & =\\frac{900}{0.9} \\\\ x & =1000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the original salary was $\\$\\,1000.$"] [17592186625583 :question/problem "Approximately $15\\%$ of the emails received in an account are spam. If there are $18$ emails in the inbox, what is the probability that at least $2$ are spam emails? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186625583 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of spam emails.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=18$ inbox emails\n\n- the probability of being a spam email is $p=15\\%=0.15$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(18,0.15).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{18}{x})(0.15)^{x}(1-0.15)^{18-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{18}{x})(0.15)^{x}(0.85)^{18-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that there are at least $2$ spam emails. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=18)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{18}{0})(0.15)^{0}(0.85)^{18-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{18}{1})(0.15)^{1}(0.85)^{18-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.85)^{18}+16(0.15)(0.85)^{17}] \\\\  & =1-0.284\\,053 \\\\  & =0.776\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186625595 :question/problem "A water tank leaked some water. After $55\\%$ of the original water leaked out, $360$ liters remained. How many liters leaked out?"] [17592186625595 :question/worked-solution "Decreasing a number by $55\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%-55\\%=45\\%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n45\\%=\\frac{45}{100}=0.45.\n$$\n\nLet the original liters of water be $x.$ So, after a $55\\%$ decrease, the new value is \n\n$$\n0.45x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $360$ liters remained. So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n0.45x=360.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.45x & =360 \\\\ x & =\\frac{360}{0.45} \\\\ x & =800\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the original amount was $800$ liters, we find the amount removed by taking $55\\%$ of $800:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{55}{100}\\cdot 800=440.\n$$\n\nSo, $440$ liters leaked out."] [17592186625597 :question/problem "In a shipment, some batteries were discarded. After $10\\%$ of the original batteries were discarded, $810$ batteries remained. How many batteries were discarded?"] [17592186625597 :question/worked-solution "Decreasing a number by $10\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%-10\\%=90\\%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n90\\%=\\frac{90}{100}=0.9.\n$$\n\nLet the original number of batteries be $x.$ So, after a $10\\%$ decrease, the new value is \n\n$$\n0.9x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $810$ batteries remained. So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n0.9x=810.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.90x & =810 \\\\ x & =\\frac{810}{0.90} \\\\ x & =900\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the original number of batteries was $900,$ we find the number removed by taking $10\\%$ of $900:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{10}{100}\\cdot 900=90.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of batteries discarded was $90.$"] [17592186625612 :question/problem "In a library, some magazines were removed. After $18\\%$ of the original magazines were removed, $410$ magazines remained. How many magazines were removed?"] [17592186625612 :question/worked-solution "Decreasing a number by $18\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%-18\\%=82\\%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n82\\%=\\frac{82}{100}=0.82.\n$$\n\nLet the original number of magazines be $x.$ So, after an $18\\%$ decrease, the new value is \n\n$$\n0.82x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $410$ magazines remained. So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n0.82x=410.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.82x & =410 \\\\ x & =\\frac{410}{0.82} \\\\ x & =500\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the original number of magazines was $500,$ we find the number removed by taking $18\\%$ of $500:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{18}{100}\\cdot 500=90.\n$$\n\nSo, $90$ magazines were removed."] [17592186626712 :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case,\n\n* the part is the number of forget-me-nots in the display, which is $210$, and\n\n* the whole is the total number of flowers, which is $280.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=210÷280=\\frac{210}{280}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{210÷70}{280÷70}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3\\times 25}{4\\times 25}=\\frac{75}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{75}{100}=75\\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $75\\%$ of the flowers Andrew has used in his display are forget-me-nots."] [17592186627674 :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the number of red marbles, which is $12,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total number of marbles, which is $25.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=12÷25=\\frac{12}{25}.\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{25}=\\frac{12\\times 4}{25\\times 4}=\\frac{48}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{48}{100}=48\\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $48\\%$ of the marbles are red."] [17592186627683 :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the number of blue marbles, which is $18,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total number of marbles, which is $30.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=18÷30=\\frac{18}{30}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{18}{30}=\\frac{18÷6}{30÷6}=\\frac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{3\\times 20}{5\\times 20}=\\frac{60}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{60}{100}=60\\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $60\\%$ of the marbles are blue."] [17592186627688 :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the number of pages Patricia read, which is $180,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total number of pages, which is $600.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=180÷600=\\frac{18}{60}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{18}{60}=\\frac{18÷6}{60÷6}=\\frac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{10}=\\frac{3\\times 10}{10\\times 10}=\\frac{30}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{30}{100}=30\\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, Patricia has read $30\\%$ of the book."] [17592186627718 :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the weight of cereal eaten, which is $14\\,\\text{g}$, and\n\n- the whole is the total weight of cereal in the box, which is $350\\,\\text{g}.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=14÷350=\\frac{14}{350}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{14}{350}=\\frac{14÷14}{350÷14}=\\frac{1}{25}.\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{25}=\\frac{1\\times 4}{25\\times 4}=\\frac{4}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{100}=4\\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, Brian ate $4\\%$ of the cereal."] [17592186627724 :question/problem "The output voltage of a particular model of laptop AC adapters is normally distributed with a mean of $19$ volts. However, $2.5\\%$ of adapters have a voltage of less than $18.86$ volts. Find the standard deviation of the output voltage of laptop AC adapters, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.900\\,$ | $\\,0.950\\,$ | $\\,0.975\\,$ | $\\,0.990\\,$ | $\\,0.995\\,$ | $\\,0.999\\,$ | $\\,0.9995\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.9600\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ | $\\,2.5758\\,$ | $\\,3.0902\\,$ | $\\,3.2905\\,$ |"] [17592186628432 :question/problem "An archer practices shooting arrows at a target. The archer has a $25\\%$ chance of hitting the bullseye. What is the probability that the archer hits the bullseye in one of the first $3$ shoots? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186628432 :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of shoots before hitting the bullseye. Since the chance of hitting the bullseye is $25\\%,$ the number of shoots required to hit the bullseye can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=25\\%=\\frac{25}{100}=0.25.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.25).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.25)^{x-1}(0.25) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{x-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of hitting the bullseye in one of the first $3$ shoots is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{1-1}(0.25)+(0.75)^{2-1}(0.25)+(0.75)^{3-1}(0.25) \\\\  & =(0.25)+(0.75)(0.25)+(0.75)^{2}(0.25) \\\\  & =[1+0.75+(0.75)^{2}](0.25) \\\\  & ≈0.578\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186628449 :question/problem "A book is being randomly chosen from a backpack and put back in until getting a math book. $35\\%$ of the books in the backpack are math books. What is the probability that a math book is chosen in one of the first $3$ tries?  Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186628449 :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of random choices made before getting a math book. Since the chance of getting a math book is $35\\%,$ the number of choices required to get a math book can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=35\\%=\\frac{35}{100}=0.35.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.35).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.35)^{x-1}(0.35) \\\\  & =(0.65)^{x-1}(0.35)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of choosing a math book in one of the first $3$ tries is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.65)^{1-1}(0.35)+(0.65)^{2-1}(0.35)+(0.65)^{3-1}(0.35) \\\\  & =(0.35)+(0.65)(0.35)+(0.65)^{2}(0.35) \\\\  & =[1+0.65+(0.65)^{2}](0.35) \\\\  & ≈0.725\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186628499 :question/problem "Billy ran some scripts on his computer. He started by running $300$ scripts, and the number of scripts remaining decreased by $2\\%$ after every minute. To the nearest integer, how many scripts were running after $4$ minutes?"] [17592186628499 :question/worked-solution "The number $2\\%$ (expressed as a decimal) is equal to $0.02.$ Therefore, a decrease of $2\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of:\n\n$$\n1-0.02=0.98\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ minute, $300\\cdot (0.98)$ scripts were running.\n\n- After $2$ minutes,  $300\\cdot (0.98)^{2}$ scripts were running.\n\n- After $3$ minutes, $300\\cdot (0.98)^{3}$ scripts were running.\n\n- After $4$ minutes, $300\\cdot (0.98)^{4}$ scripts were running.\n\nEvaluating the last expression gives\n\n$$\n300\\cdot (0.98)^{4}≈277.\n$$\n\nSo, there were $277$ scripts running after $4$ minutes."] [17592186629255 :question/problem "A smartphone manufacturer tests its screens for tiny scratches before assembly. On average, $18\\%$ of the screens are found to be scratched. Each screen is tested independently of the others. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that the first scratched screen is found \non the $4$th or later tested screen is{{field-1}}."] [17592186629255 :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of screens tested until the first scratched screen is found. Since the chance that any single screen is scratched is $18\\%,$ the number of screens tested until the first scratched screen is found can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=18\\%=\\frac{18}{100}=0.18.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.18).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.18)^{x-1}(0.18) \\\\  & =(0.82)^{x-1}(0.18)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X\\ge 4),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 4)=1-P(X<4)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.82)^{1-1}(0.18)+(0.82)^{2-1}(0.18)+(0.82)^{3-1}(0.18) \\\\  & =[1+(0.82)+(0.82)^{2}](0.18) \\\\  & ≈0.449,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability that the first scratched screen is found on the $4$th or later tested screen is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 4) & =1-P(X<4) \\\\  & ≈1-0.449 \\\\  & ≈0.551,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186629274 :question/problem "The percentage $p$ at which a pool is filled is directly proportional to the filling time $t,$ measured in hours ($h$). When the filling time is $t=8\\,h,$ the percentage fill is $p=60\\%.$ Find the filling time when the percentage is $p=100\\%.$"] [17592186629274 :question/worked-solution "The percentage fill $p$ is directly proportional to the time $t,$ so we have\n\n$$\np=kt.\n$$\n\nWe are told that when the time is $t=8\\,h,$ the percentage fill is $p=60\\%.$  We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =k\\cdot (8) \\\\ 60 & =8k \\\\ \\frac{60}{8} & =k \\\\ 7.5 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\np=7.5t.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the time when the percentage is $p=100\\%,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{100}{7.5}=13+\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time when $p=100\\%$  is $t=13\\,\\text{h}\\,20\\,\\text{min}.$"] [17592186629342 :question/problem "David is randomly picking a crayon from a box and putting it back in until he gets a green crayon. Approximately, $15\\%$ of the crayons in the box are green. What is the probability that the $5$th crayon is the first green crayon picked? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186629342 :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of picked crayons until a green crayon is obtained. Since the chance to get a green crayon is $15\\%,$ the number of picked crayons until a green crayon is obtained can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=15\\%=\\frac{15}{100}=0.15.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.15).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.15)^{x-1}(0.15) \\\\  & =(0.85)^{x-1}(0.15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the $5$th picked crayon is the first green crayon is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =(0.85)^{5-1}(0.15) \\\\  & =(0.85)^{4}(0.15) \\\\  & ≈0.078\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186630185 :question/problem "A particular town started with $67\\,000$ inhabitants. The population, $P$ of the town after $t$ years, is given by the formula\n\n$$\nP=67\\,000\\cdot (\\text{___})^{t}.\n$$\n\nIf the population of the town reduces at a rate of $4\\%$ per year, then what is the missing value in the expression for $P?$"] [17592186630185 :question/worked-solution "The population of the town reduces at a rate of $4\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is:\n\n$$\ny(t)=a(1-r)^{t}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=67\\,000$ is the initial number of inhabitants.\n\n- $r=0.04$ is the decay rate ($4\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $P$ is\n\n$$\np(t)=67\\,000\\cdot (1-0.04)^{t}=67\\,000\\cdot (0.96)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $0.96.$"] [17592186630210 :question/problem "Due to sales, Carol's stock of laptops has decreased at a rate of $3\\%$ every day. If she initially had $680$ laptops in stock, then which function correctly represents $y(t),$ the number of laptops remaining in stock after $t$ days?"] [17592186630210 :question/worked-solution "The formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=680$ is the initial number of laptops.\n\n- $r=0.03$ is the decay rate ($3\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in days.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=680\\cdot (1-0.03)^{t}=680\\cdot (0.97)^{t}.\n$$"] [17592186630261 :question/problem "The monthly profit $p(t)$ of a certain business after $t$ months is given by the formula \n\n$$\np(t)=12\\,520\\cdot (\\text{___})^{t}.\n$$\n\nIf the initial monthly profit was $\\$\\,12\\,520$, and the profit is expected to decrease by $17\\%$ each month, then what is the missing value in the expression for $p(t)?$"] [17592186630261 :question/worked-solution "The formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\np(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=12\\,520$ is the initial monthly profit.\n\n- $r=0.17$ is the decay rate ($17\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in months.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $p(t)$ is\n\n$$\np(t)=12\\,520\\cdot (1-0.17)^{t}=12\\,520\\cdot (0.83)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $0.83.$"] [17592186630908 :question/worked-solution "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot (1-r)^{t},$ where:\n\n- $a=20$ represents the initial mass of the radioactive sample.\n\n- $1-r=0.5,$ where $r$ is the decay rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time since the start of the observations, in thousands of years.\n\nSolving for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n1-r=0.5\\,⟹\\,r=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, $0.5$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $50\\%.$ \n\nSo, the base of the exponential expression $0.5$ means that the radioactive sample loses $50\\%$ of its mass every thousand years."] [17592186630922 :question/worked-solution "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot (1-r)^{t},$ where:\n\n- $a=2\\,500$ represents the initial number of parrots.\n\n- $1-r=0.95,$ where $r$ is the decay rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time in years since the island was inhabited.\n\nSolving for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n1-r=0.95\\,⟹\\,r=0.05.\n$$\n\nNow, $0.05$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $5\\%.$ \n\nSo, the base of the exponential expression $0.95$ means that the number of parrots decreases by $5\\%$ every year."] [17592186630935 :question/worked-solution "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot (1-r)^{t},$ where:\n\n- $a=300$ represents the initial value of the cell phone (in dollars).\n\n- $1-r=0.3,$ where $r$ is the decay rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time since the start of the observation, in years.\n\nSolving for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n1-r=0.3\\,⟹\\,r=0.7.\n$$\n\nNow, $0.7$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $70\\%.$ \n\nSo, the base of the exponential expression $0.3$ means that the value of the cell phone decreases by $70\\%$ each year."] [17592186630947 :question/problem "![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=150$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =3.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=5,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*"] [17592186630947 :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=150\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.10\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{150}} \\\\ 5\\pm 0.403\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186630989 :question/problem "The lifetime of a laptop produced by a particular company is distributed with a population standard deviation of $\\sigma =6$ months. A sample of $90$ laptops was examined, and it was found that their mean lifetime is $42$ months. Find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean lifetime $\\mu$ of the entire laptop production. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |"] [17592186630989 :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=90\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=42,\\,n=90,\\,\\sigma =6.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/2f/2fc6327df99c6b88508b0c4de2e4edf6c5521974f05e2e2f02d09786937285a7.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}42\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{90}} \\\\ 42\\pm 1.04\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186630996 :question/worked-solution "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot (1-r)^{t},$ where:\n\n- $a=20\\,000$ represents the initial value of the car .\n\n- $1-r=0.2,$ where $r$ is the decay rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time since the start of the observations, in years.\n\nSolving for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n1-r=0.2\\,⟹\\,r=0.8.\n$$\n\nNow, $0.8$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $80\\%.$ \n\nSo, the base of the exponential expression $0.2$ means that the car loses $80\\%$ of its value every year."] [17592186632665 :question/problem "![](images/28/28f56656e191e342a4815ba74bdd29cba5247fba7d8ab0feca8ffbe0b785ab8c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=38$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma =20.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=125,$ find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*"] [17592186632665 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 125\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{20}{\\sqrt{38}} \\\\  & 125\\pm 6.359\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186632674 :question/problem "Roy has $128$ pieces of candy that he shares with his friends. The number of candy pieces in Roy's bag decreases by $37.5\\%$ every day. How many days will it take until there are only $50$ pieces of candy left in Roy's bag?"] [17592186632674 :question/worked-solution "The number of pieces of candy $y(t)$ decays exponentially with a decay rate of $37.5\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=128$ is the initial number of candy.\n\n- $r=0.375$ is the decay rate ($37.5\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in days.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=128\\cdot (1-0.375)^{t}=128\\cdot {(0.625)}^{t}.\n$$\n\nSince $0.625=\\frac{5}{8},$ this is equivalent to\n\n$$\ny(t)=128\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe want to know how many days it takes for the number of pieces of candy to decrease to $50$, so we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n128\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{t}=50.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}128\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{t} & =50 \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{t} & =\\frac{50}{128} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{t} & =\\frac{25}{64} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{t} & =\\frac{{5}^{2}}{{8}^{2}} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{t} & ={\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, it takes $2$ days for the number of candy pieces to decrease to $50.$"] [17592186632687 :question/problem "A vacation house was first valued at $\\$100\\,000.$ Every year its value decreases by $10\\%.$ How many years will pass until the house value is $\\$72\\,900?$"] [17592186632687 :question/worked-solution "The vacation house's value $v(t)$ decays exponentially with a decay rate of $10\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nv(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=100\\,000$ is the initial value of the house, in dollars.\n\n- $r=0.1$ is the decay rate ($10\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $v$ is\n\n$$\nv(t)=100\\,000(1-0.1)^{t}=100\\,000(0.9)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSince $0.9=\\frac{9}{10},$ this is equivalent to\n\n$$\nv(t)=100\\,000{\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe want to know how many years it takes for the house's value to decrease to $\\$72\\,900,$ so we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n100\\,000{\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{t}=72\\,900.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100\\,000{\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{t} & =72\\,900 \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{t} & =\\frac{72\\,900}{100\\,000} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{t} & =\\frac{729}{1000} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{t} & =\\frac{{9}^{3}}{{10}^{3}} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{t} & ={\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{3} \\\\ t & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, it takes $3$ years for the vacation house value to decrease to $\\$72\\,900.$"] [17592186632688 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & x \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\text{det}(B)=2$, what is the value of $x?$"] [17592186632709 :question/problem "A gym is losing $5\\%$ of its members per month. In its most recent month, the gym counted $1\\,943$ members. How many members were there six months ago?"] [17592186632709 :question/worked-solution "The number of members $y(t)$ decays exponentially with a decay rate of $5\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a$ is the initial number of members (which is what we're trying to find).\n\n- $r=0.05$ is the decay rate ($5\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in months.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a\\cdot (1-0.05)^{t}=a\\cdot (0.95)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe know that there were $1\\,943$ members after $6$ months. Therefore,\n\n$$\na\\cdot (0.95)^{6}=1\\,943.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{1\\,943}{(0.95)^{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,943}{0.735...} \\\\  & ≈2643\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186632710 :question/problem "Harriet is studying an online course that has $36$ topics in total. She has planned her schedule so that the number of incomplete topics decreases by $13\\%$ every week. What equation determines the number of weeks $t$ that it will take for there to be only $12$ topics left to complete."] [17592186632710 :question/worked-solution "The number of incomplete topics $y(t)$ decreases exponentially with a decay rate of $13\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=36$ is the initial number of incomplete topics.\n\n- $r=0.13$ is the decay rate ($13\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in weeks.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=36\\cdot (1-0.13)^{t}=36\\cdot (0.87)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSince we want to know the number of weeks it takes for the number of incomplete topics to reduce to $12,$ we need to solve the equation $y(t)=12.$ This gives\n\n$$\n36\\cdot (0.87)^{t}=12.\n$$"] [17592186632724 :question/problem "During the Christmas season, a Christmas tree is lit with $10\\,000$ lights. Since then, the number of lit lights decreases by $20\\%$ every week. How many weeks will pass until there are only $4\\,096$ lit lights?"] [17592186632724 :question/worked-solution "The number of lit lights $x(t)$ decays exponentially with a decay rate of $20\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nx(t)=a\\cdot (1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=10\\,000$ is the initial number of lit lights.\n\n- $r=0.2$ is the decay rate ($20\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in weeks.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $x$ is\n\n$$\nx(t)=10\\,000\\cdot (1-0.2)^{t}=10\\,000\\cdot {(0.8)}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe want to know how many weeks it takes for the number of lit lights to decrease to $4\\,096,$ and so we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n10\\,000\\cdot {(0.8)}^{t}=4\\,096.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\,000\\cdot {(0.8)}^{t} & =4\\,096 \\\\ 10\\,000\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{8}{10}\\right)}^{t} & =4\\,096 \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{8}{10}\\right)}^{t} & =\\frac{4\\,096}{10\\,000} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{8}{10}\\right)}^{t} & =\\frac{{8}^{4}}{{10}^{4}} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{8}{10}\\right)}^{t} & ={\\left(\\frac{8}{10}\\right)}^{4} \\\\ t & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, it takes $4$ weeks for the number of lit lights to decrease to $4\\,096.$"] [17592186632728 :question/problem "The volume of water in a particular lake is known to decrease by $5\\%$ every year. Currently, there are $9\\,025\\,{\\text{km}}^{3}$ of water in the lake. How much water was in the lake $2$ years ago?"] [17592186632728 :question/worked-solution "The amount of water in the lake $y(t)$ decays exponentially with a decay rate of $5\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a$ is the initial amount of water in the lake (which is what we're trying to find).\n\n- $r=0.05$ is the decay rate ($5\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a\\cdot (1-0.05)^{t}=a\\cdot (0.95)^{t},\n$$\n\nWe know that the amount of water in a lake was $9\\,025$ after $2$ years. Therefore,\n\n$$\na\\cdot (0.95)^{2}=9\\,025\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{9\\,025}{(0.95)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\,025}{0.9025} \\\\  & =10\\,000\\,{\\text{km}}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186632739 :question/problem "A car loses $18\\%$ of its value every year from the time it was bought. If Kennedy bought his car for $\\$14\\,800,$ what equation should be solved to determine the number of years $t$ it will take for the price to decrease to $\\$2\\,360?$"] [17592186632739 :question/worked-solution "The value of the car $v(t)$ decreases exponentially with a decay rate of $18\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nv(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=14\\,800$ is the purchase price.\n\n- $r=0.18$ is the decay rate ($18\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nSince we want to know how many years it takes to decrease to $\\$2\\,360,$ we need to solve the equation $v(t)=2\\,360.$ This gives\n\n$$\n14\\,800\\cdot (0.82)^{t}=2\\,360.\n$$"] [17592186633506 :question/problem "![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=18$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=21$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=6.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=19$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=23,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*"] [17592186633506 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=19-23=-4\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{5}^{2}}{18}+\\frac{{6}^{2}}{21}}≈2.898\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $90\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -4\\pm 2.898\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186633524 :question/problem "![](images/64/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=18$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=11$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=15$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=8.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=14$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=19,$ find a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*"] [17592186633524 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=14-19=-5\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{11}^{2}}{18}+\\frac{{8}^{2}}{15}}≈8.539\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -5\\pm 8.539\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186633531 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=38$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=2.3$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=64$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.2.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=42$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=32,$ find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*"] [17592186633531 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=42-32=10\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{2.3}^{2}}{38}+\\frac{{3.2}^{2}}{64}}≈1.072\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 10\\pm 1.072\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186633533 :question/problem "In a particular town, medium-breed dogs are fed two different diets, X and Y. A study finds that the weights of the dogs fed on diets X and Y have standard deviations of $4\\,lb$ and $6\\,lb,$ respectively. A veterinarian wants to determine whether the population mean weight ${\\mu }_{x}$ of the dogs fed diet X is the same as the population mean weight ${\\mu }_{y}$ of the dogs fed diet Y. He constructs a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nThe veterinarian sampled $45$ dogs fed diet X and $50$ dogs fed diet Y and found that their mean weights were $29.5\\,\\text{lb}$ and $26.8\\,\\text{lb},$ respectively. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $2.7\\pm 2.672.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $99\\%$ confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{y}>{\\mu }_{x}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |"] [17592186633533 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=29.5-26.8=2.7\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=2.576:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{4}^{2}}{45}+\\frac{{6}^{2}}{50}}≈2.672\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2.7\\pm 2.672\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $2.7\\pm 2.672.$\n\n- Statement II is true. All values within the confidence interval are positive:\n\n$$\n(2.7-2.672,\\,2.7+2.672)=(0.028,\\,5.372)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since all values within the confidence interval are positive, this suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186633566 :question/problem "![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=25$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=4$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=16$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=13$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=8,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*"] [17592186633566 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=13-8=5\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{4}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{2}^{2}}{16}}≈1.552\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $90\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 5\\pm 1.552\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186633585 :question/problem "![](images/69/693576483310a4656435ea4a3d8d9652b8de563d26a87c7c80fb902a3bef1e29.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=24$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=4$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=36$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=9.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=21$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=17,$ find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*"] [17592186633585 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=21-17=4\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{4}^{2}}{24}+\\frac{{9}^{2}}{36}}≈3.347\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 4\\pm 3.347\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [1759218