ute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{5.6}^{2}}{91}+\\frac{{2.1}^{2}}{47}}≈1.706\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 11\\pm 1.706\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186633592 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=68$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=5.4$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=82$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=13$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=32,$ find a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*"] [17592186633592 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=13-32=-19\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{5.4}^{2}}{68}+\\frac{{4}^{2}}{82}}≈2.035\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -19\\pm 2.035\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186633603 :question/problem "The weights of tomato varieties X and Y are known to be normally distributed with standard deviations of ${\\sigma }_{x}=15\\,\\text{g}$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=12\\,\\text{g},$ respectively. A farmer wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of weights of variety X is smaller than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of weights of variety Y. They decide to construct a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nThe farmer sampled $20$ tomatoes of variety X and $25$ tomatoes of variety Y and found that their mean weights are $99\\,\\text{g}$ and $104\\,\\text{g},$ respectively. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The end-points of the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $-5\\pm 8.084.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $95\\%$ confidence interval are negative.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}<{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |"] [17592186633603 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=99-104=-5\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.960:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.960\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{15}^{2}}{20}+\\frac{{12}^{2}}{25}}≈8.084\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -5\\pm 8.084\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $-5\\pm 8.084.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Not all values within the confidence interval are negative:\n\n$$\n(-5-8.084,\\,-5+8.084)=(-13.084,\\,3.084)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the confidence interval contains zero, it remains possible that ${\\mu }_{x}={\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186634560 :question/problem "Albert kept records of his car's fuel efficiency. After the first $49$ times he filled up the tank, he found the mean fuel efficiency was $32.5$ miles per gallon ($\\text{mpg}$) with a sample standard deviation of $4.2\\,\\text{mpg}.$ Find a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean  $\\text{mpg}$  $\\mu$ of Albert's car.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $47$ | $\\,1.300\\,$ | $\\,1.678\\,$ | $\\,2.012\\,$ | $\\,2.408\\,$ | $\\,2.685\\,$ |\n| $48$ | $\\,1.299\\,$ | $\\,1.677\\,$ | $\\,2.011\\,$ | $\\,2.407\\,$ | $\\,2.682\\,$ |\n| $49$ | $\\,1.299\\,$ | $\\,1.677\\,$ | $\\,2.010\\,$ | $\\,2.405\\,$ | $\\,2.680\\,$ |"] [17592186634560 :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=49\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where  \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=32.5\\,\\text{mpg},\\,n=49,\\,s=4.2\\,\\text{mpg}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{48,0.005}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{48,0.005})=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=49-1=48$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/23/2348635fd9697e8610533dbbfc019ba5da5b817381ce1c71cbfe6d74476deea7.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{48,0.005}=2.682:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $47$ | $\\,1.300\\,$ | $\\,1.678\\,$ | $\\,2.012\\,$ | $\\,2.408\\,$ | $\\,2.685\\,$ |\n| $48$ | $\\,1.299\\,$ | $\\,1.677\\,$ | $\\,2.011\\,$ | $\\,2.407\\,$ | $2.682$ |\n| $49$ | $\\,1.299\\,$ | $\\,1.677\\,$ | $\\,2.010\\,$ | $\\,2.405\\,$ | $\\,2.680\\,$ |\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32.5 & \\pm 2.682\\cdot \\frac{4.2}{\\sqrt{49}}\\,\\text{mpg} \\\\ 32.5 & \\pm 1.609\\,\\text{mpg}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186634574 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=25$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $68$ and the sample standard deviation is $4,$ find a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.797)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $24$ degrees of freedom.*"] [17592186634574 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=68,\\,n=25,\\,s=4.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.797)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{24,0.005}=2.797.\n$$\n\n![](images/4f/4f9a0b7c9ec55a5c061f709b2d6fa02c3f760ccd66faab24c1201c4cf48bb18c.png)\n\nTherefore, a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 68\\pm 2.797\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & 68\\pm 2.238\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186634586 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=289$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $5,$ and the sample standard deviation is $1.36,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.65)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $288$ degrees of freedom.*"] [17592186634586 :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=289\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=5,\\,n=289,\\,s=1.36.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.65)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{288,0.05}=1.65.\n$$\n\n![](images/79/7950d5d709cdd831192edf0897eafef040e1207503d13b40090624c058cfab2d.png)\n\nTherefore, the $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & \\pm 1.65\\cdot \\frac{1.36}{\\sqrt{289}} \\\\ 5 & \\pm 0.132\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186634590 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=144$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $77.6,$ and the sample standard deviation is $6,$ find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.977)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $143$ degrees of freedom.*"] [17592186634590 :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=144\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*.  \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=77.6,\\,n=144,\\,s=6.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.977)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{143,0.025}=1.977.\n$$\n\n![](images/02/026f481acfd1d933b7467ac302cd9cda2ef9873f752255682343f6a970dec285.png)\n\nTherefore, the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}77.6 & \\pm 1.977\\cdot \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{144}} \\\\ 77.6 & \\pm 0.989\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186634600 :question/problem "A random sample of $81$ students from a certain high school yields a mean height of $1.68\\,\\text{m}$ with a sample standard deviation of $0.12\\,\\text{m}.$ Find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population  mean height $\\mu$ of all students in the school.       \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $79$ | $\\,1.292\\,$ | $\\,1.664\\,$ | $\\,1.990\\,$ | $\\,2.374\\,$ | $\\,2.640\\,$ |\n| $80$ | $\\,1.292\\,$ | $\\,1.664\\,$ | $\\,1.990\\,$ | $\\,2.374\\,$ | $\\,2.639\\,$ |\n| $81$ | $\\,1.292\\,$ | $\\,1.664\\,$ | $\\,1.990\\,$ | $\\,2.373\\,$ | $\\,2.638\\,$ |"] [17592186634600 :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=81\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=1.68\\,\\text{m}\\,n=81,\\,s=0.12\\,\\text{m}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{80,0.025}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{80,0.025})=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=81-1=80$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/d5/d513fbeee39a42a5e897c1b171d7e820196066517c5aa1c6c12549a98ce00ddd.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{80,0.025}=1.99:$  \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $79$ | $\\,1.292\\,$ | $\\,1.664\\,$ | $\\,1.990\\,$ | $\\,2.374\\,$ | $\\,2.640\\,$ |\n| $80$ | $\\,1.292\\,$ | $\\,1.664\\,$ | $1.990$ | $\\,2.374\\,$ | $\\,2.639\\,$ |\n| $81$ | $\\,1.292\\,$ | $\\,1.664\\,$ | $\\,1.990\\,$ | $\\,2.373\\,$ | $\\,2.638\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.68 & \\pm 1.99\\cdot \\frac{0.12}{\\sqrt{81}}\\,\\text{m} \\\\ 1.68 & \\pm 0.027\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186634610 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=9$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $51$ and the sample standard deviation is $5,$ find a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>3.355)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $8$ degrees of freedom.*"] [17592186634610 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=51,\\,n=9,\\,s=5.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>3.355)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{8,0.005}=3.355.\n$$\n\n![](images/84/84f060ec1a5e8a8b3db0c0866ebef9db1c242e944717a5abfd28447e0eddb9be.png)\n\nTherefore, a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}51 & \\pm 3.355\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\ 51 & \\pm 5.592\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186634614 :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of $35$ language students, where $80\\%$ of the students in the language school speak more than two languages.\n\nII. A sample from a list of $20$ newspaper subscribers, where $50\\%$ of the subscribers read the newspaper online.\n\nIII. A sample of $45$ clients of a bank in a town, where $90\\%$ of the clients have a savings account.\n\n*Hint: In each case, you may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [17592186634614 :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nWe're given that the population is significantly larger than the sample size in each case. Therefore, we may assume that the first condition is satisfied in each case.\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =35(0.8) \\\\  & =28 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =35(1-0.8) \\\\  & =7 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =20(0.5) \\\\  & =10 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =20(1-0.5) \\\\  & =10 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =45(0.9) \\\\  & =40.5 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =45(1-0.9) \\\\  & =4.5 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186634618 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=250$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $67.5,$ and the sample standard deviation is $16,$ find a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.596)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $249$ degrees of freedom.*"] [17592186634618 :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=250\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=67.5,\\,n=250,\\,s=16.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.596)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{249,0.005}=2.596.\n$$\n\n![](images/0c/0c4c4953908776101b2273c8152ec370be422b0a61b755a2e4cdd98bc68bfe92.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}67.5 & \\pm 2.596\\cdot \\frac{16}{\\sqrt{250}} \\\\ 67.5 & \\pm 2.627\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186634630 :question/problem "![](images/34/34f763e44de32f1154705cb184c38340c15fa5bfe4fcf8d7c037905014f70496.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=9$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $4.2,$ and the sample standard deviation is $2,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.86)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $8$ degrees of freedom,  as shown in the diagram above.*"] [17592186634630 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=4.2,\\,n=9,\\,s=2.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.86)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{8,0.05}=1.86.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4.2 & \\pm 1.86\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\ 4.2 & \\pm 1.24\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186634638 :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=36$ from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $80\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=31$ from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $12\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=59$ from a population of size $N=500$ in which $80\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic."] [17592186634638 :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =36 \\\\  & \\le 5\\%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =36(0.8) \\\\  & =28.8 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =36(1-0.8) \\\\  & =7.2 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =31 \\\\  & \\le 5\\%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =31(0.12) \\\\  & =3.72 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $np≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =59, \\\\ 5\\%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 500 \\\\  & =25 \\\\  & <59.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is more than $5\\%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186634639 :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of $15$ athletes in a competition, where $20\\%$ of the world's athletes run $100$ meters in less than $10$ seconds.\n\nII. A sample of $100$ water bottles dispensed by a machine, where it is known that $96\\%$ of the bottles dispensed by the machine are one-liter water bottles.\n\nIII. A sample of $70$ calls in a call center, given that $60\\%$ of the calls received are complaints.\n\n*Hint: In each case, you may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [17592186634639 :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nWe're given that the population is significantly larger than the sample size in each case. Therefore, we may assume that the first condition is satisfied in each case.\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =15(0.2) \\\\  & =3 \\\\  & ≯5\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $np≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100(0.96) \\\\  & =96 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =100(1-0.96) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =70(0.6) \\\\  & =42 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =70(1-0.6) \\\\  & =28 \\\\  & >5\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [17592186634642 :question/problem "A soda company has a machine that fills one-liter bottles of soda. An inspector knows that $65\\%$ of the filled bottles contain less than one liter of soda. Find a normal approximation for the probability that in a random sample of $35$ bottles, fewer than $14$ of them contain less than one liter of soda. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*"] [17592186634650 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=100$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $20,$ and the sample standard deviation is $15,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.66)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $99$ degrees of freedom.*"] [17592186634650 :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=100\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=20,\\,n=100,\\,s=15.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.66)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{99,0.05}=1.66.\n$$\n\n![](images/b1/b194e10edf36bd3b01f674566e8238995b835500f4a32df8d346470835ad86fc.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20 & \\pm 1.66\\cdot \\frac{15}{\\sqrt{100}} \\\\ 20 & \\pm 2.49\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186634653 :question/problem "A company takes four one-cup samples of its branded white rice and measures the number of calories in each cup.\nThe sample mean is $199\\,\\text{Cal}$ with a sample standard deviation of $1.2\\,\\text{Cal}.$\nAssuming that the caloric content of the rice is normally distributed, find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean of the number of calories $\\mu$ in a cup of white rice of that particular brand.   \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $\\,1.886\\,$ | $\\,2.920\\,$ | $\\,4.303\\,$ | $\\,6.965\\,$ | $\\,9.925\\,$ |\n| $3$ | $\\,1.638\\,$ | $\\,2.353\\,$ | $\\,3.182\\,$ | $\\,4.541\\,$ | $\\,5.841\\,$ |\n| $4$ | $\\,1.533\\,$ | $\\,2.132\\,$ | $\\,2.776\\,$ | $\\,3.747\\,$ | $\\,4.604\\,$ |"] [17592186634653 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma .$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=199\\,\\text{Cal},\\,n=4,\\,s=1.2\\,\\text{Cal}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{3,0.025}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{3,0.025})=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=4-1=3$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/26/26d34739ef709e83536422b50e3f5719be2a66f7d526d3d3a485b8c702d40ff1.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{3,0.025}=3.182:$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $\\,1.886\\,$ | $\\,2.920\\,$ | $\\,4.303\\,$ | $\\,6.965\\,$ | $\\,9.925\\,$ |\n| $3$ | $\\,1.638\\,$ | $\\,2.353\\,$ | $3.182$ | $\\,4.541\\,$ | $\\,5.841\\,$ |\n| $4$ | $\\,1.533\\,$ | $\\,2.132\\,$ | $\\,2.776\\,$ | $\\,3.747\\,$ | $\\,4.604\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}199 & \\pm 3.182\\cdot \\frac{1.2}{\\sqrt{4}}\\,\\text{Cal} \\\\ 199 & \\pm 1.909\\,\\text{Cal}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186634654 :question/problem "![](images/b6/b64bea76519c54941db2e345ab83387e76a22f698c354a3028cebef6aa27cb79.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=25$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $70,$ and the sample standard deviation is $10,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.711)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $24$ degrees of freedom,  as shown in the diagram above.*"] [17592186634654 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=70,\\,n=25,\\,s=10.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.711)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{24,0.05}=1.711.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}70 & \\pm 1.711\\cdot \\frac{10}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\ 70 & \\pm 3.422\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186634670 :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=40$ from a population of size $N=2\\,000$ in which $15\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=40$ from a population of size $N=200$ in which $90\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=40$ from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $5\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic."] [17592186634670 :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40 \\\\  & \\le 5\\%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 2\\,000 \\\\  & =100\\,✓ \\\\ np & =40(0.15) \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =40(1-0.15) \\\\  & =34 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40, \\\\ 5\\%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 200 \\\\  & =10 \\\\  & <40.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is more than $5\\%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40 \\\\  & \\le 5\\%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =40(0.05) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $np≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186634676 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=81$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $12.8,$ and the sample standard deviation is $6.3,$ find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.99)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $80$ degrees of freedom.*"] [17592186634676 :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=81\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*.  \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=12.8,\\,n=81,\\,s=6.3.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.99)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{80,0.025}=1.99.\n$$\n\n![](images/67/674d178231a62144a000f4fb37fd356ffdd1848c3a941cbab1d368aff5343858.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12.8 & \\pm 1.99\\cdot \\frac{6.3}{\\sqrt{81}} \\\\ 12.8 & \\pm 1.393\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186634677 :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of $32$ customers at a clothing store, where $60\\%$ of the customers stay at the store for at least $25$ minutes.\n\nII. A sample of $20$ oranges in the supermarket, where $80\\%$ of the oranges weigh more than $130\\,\\text{g}.$\n\nIII. A sample of $50$ newborns in a town, given that $40\\%$ of the newborns in the country weigh more than $21$ inches long.\n\n*Hint: In each case, you may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [17592186634677 :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nWe're given that the population is significantly larger than the sample size in each case. Therefore, we may assume that the first condition is satisfied in each case.\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =32(0.6) \\\\  & =19.2 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =32(1-0.6) \\\\  & =12.8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =20(0.8) \\\\  & =16 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =20(1-0.8) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we cannot approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50(0.4) \\\\  & =20 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50(1-0.4) \\\\  & =30 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186634685 :question/problem "![](images/52/52f02ef08e5368a71a0dab8c28266091c7235b42dcc03f23b83d59297c111635.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=16$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $48,$ and the sample standard deviation is $8,$ find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.131)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $15$ degrees of freedom,  as shown in the diagram above.*"] [17592186634685 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=48,\\,n=16,\\,s=8.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.131)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{15,0.025}=2.131.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}48 & \\pm 2.131\\cdot \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\ 48 & \\pm 4.262\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186635472 :question/problem "A survey of $240$ randomly selected high school students shows that $144$ of the respondents play some sport. Find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the national proportion of high school students who play some sport.   \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$* \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |"] [17592186635472 :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all high school students,\n\n- some of the respondents play some sport, and some don't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=240,$ and\n\n- the proportion of students from the sample who play some sport can be found as follows: \n\n$$\n\\widehat{p\\,}=\\frac{144}{240}=0.6\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=240\\cdot 0.6=144>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=240\\cdot (1-0.6)=96>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/2e/2ed94fdbb0eb4cc1dda3720592488546a02e157598e77a70fbd02d916906b56f.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.6\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.6(1-0.6)}{240}} \\\\  & 0.6\\pm 0.052\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186635506 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=144$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that $50\\%$ of the sample has the characteristic, find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*"] [17592186635506 :question/worked-solution "Since $n=144$ and $\\widehat{\\,p}=50\\%=0.5,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=144\\cdot 0.5=72>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=144\\cdot (1-0.5)=72>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\n![](images/68/6867cf1d5e64f785003037162c6e62d0531d6c4dd8330adf139f8ae8ac46082b.png)\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.5\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.5(1-0.5)}{144}} \\\\  & 0.5\\pm 0.082\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186635527 :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f0308aa9c39034a938a5f5c63ca049ab21a47fddc20b592d660a70254990ff0.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=60$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.3,$ find a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*"] [17592186635527 :question/worked-solution "Since $n=60$ and $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.3,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=60\\cdot 0.3=18>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=60\\cdot (1-0.3)=42>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.3\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.3(1-0.3)}{60}} \\\\  & 0.3\\pm 0.152\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186635530 :question/problem "A survey of $250$ randomly selected people living in a city revealed that $200$ of them own a cell phone. Find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the proportion of people living in the city that own a cellular phone. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |"] [17592186635530 :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of people living in a certain city,\n\n- some people own a cell phone, and some don't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=250,$ and\n\n- the proportion of people from the sample who own a cell phone can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{p\\,}=\\frac{200}{250}=0.8\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=250\\cdot 0.8=200>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=250\\cdot (1-0.8)=50>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/2e/2ed94fdbb0eb4cc1dda3720592488546a02e157598e77a70fbd02d916906b56f.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.8\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.8(1-0.8)}{250}} \\\\  & 0.8\\pm 0.042\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186635531 :question/problem "A survey conducted by a travel company found that $240$ out of $600$ randomly selected adult Americans would like to visit Greece at some point in their lives. Find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the proportion of U.S. adults who would like to travel to Greece at some point in their lives.  \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |"] [17592186635531 :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- The population consists of all adults in the United States,\n\n- some of the adults interviewed would like to visit Greece at some point in their lives, and some don't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=600,$ and\n\n- the proportion of people from the sample that say they would like to travel to Greece at some point in their lives can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{p\\,}=\\frac{240}{600}=0.4\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=600\\cdot 0.4=240>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=600\\cdot (1-0.4)=360>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f58f278f66244732c379f3ad0f064227298915b8e7c364b91b8766bb8789c1ea.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.96:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.4\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.4(1-0.4)}{600}} \\\\  & 0.4\\pm 0.039\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186635533 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=125$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that $80\\%$ of the sample has the characteristic, find a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*"] [17592186635533 :question/worked-solution "Since $n=80$ and $\\widehat{\\,p}=80\\%=0.8,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=125\\cdot 0.8=100>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=125\\cdot (1-0.8)=25>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\n![](images/8f/8f0308aa9c39034a938a5f5c63ca049ab21a47fddc20b592d660a70254990ff0.png)\n\nTherefore, a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.8\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.8(1-0.8)}{125}} \\\\  & 0.8\\pm 0.092\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186635539 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=80$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that $10\\%$ of the sample has the characteristic, find a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*"] [17592186635539 :question/worked-solution "Since $n=80$ and $\\widehat{\\,p}=10\\%=0.1,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=80\\cdot 0.1=8>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=80\\cdot (1-0.1)=72>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\n![](images/8f/8f0308aa9c39034a938a5f5c63ca049ab21a47fddc20b592d660a70254990ff0.png)\n\nTherefore, a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.1\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.1(1-0.1)}{80}} \\\\  & 0.1\\pm 0.086\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186635550 :question/problem "A survey of $200$ randomly selected people registered to vote in an upcoming gubernatorial election reveals that $92$ of them will vote for the incumbent governor. Find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the statewide proportion of people who will vote for this candidate.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |"] [17592186635550 :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all people registered to vote in an upcoming gubernatorial election,\n\n- some people will vote for the incumbent governor, and some won't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=200,$ and\n\n- the proportion of people from the sample who will vote for the incumbent governor can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{p\\,}=\\frac{92}{200}=0.46\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=200\\cdot 0.46=92>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=200\\cdot (1-0.46)=108>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/2e/2ed94fdbb0eb4cc1dda3720592488546a02e157598e77a70fbd02d916906b56f.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.46\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.46(1-0.46)}{200}} \\\\  & 0.46\\pm 0.058\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186635568 :question/problem "A high school teacher surveys a random sample of $104$ students about new statewide legislation regarding school uniforms and finds that $78$ of them are against the new legislation. Find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the statewide proportion of students who are against the new legislation.  \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$* \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |"] [17592186635568 :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data: \n\n- the population consists of all high school students in a certain state,\n\n- some of the students selected are against the new statewide legislation, and some are not,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=104,$ and\n\n- the proportion of students from the sample who are against the new legislation can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{p\\,}=\\frac{78}{104}=0.75\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=104\\cdot 0.75=78>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=104\\cdot (1-0.75)=26>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f58f278f66244732c379f3ad0f064227298915b8e7c364b91b8766bb8789c1ea.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.96:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.75\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.75(1-0.75)}{104}} \\\\  & 0.75\\pm 0.083\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186636433 :question/problem "An online learning platform recently launched with an initial $125$ users. If the number of users grows by $40\\%$ each week, how many users will there be in three weeks?\n\nIn $3$ weeks, there will be\n{{field-1}}\nusers."] [17592186636433 :question/worked-solution "The online learning platform starts with $125$ users, which increases by $40\\%$ each week.\n\n$40\\%$ as a decimal is $0.4.$ Therefore, an increase of $40\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.4=1.4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ week, the online learning platform has $125\\cdot (1.4)$ users.\n\n- After $2$ weeks, the online learning platform will have $125\\cdot (1.4)^{2}$ users.\n\n- After $3$ weeks, the online learning platform will have $125\\cdot (1.4)^{3}$ users.\n\nEvaluating the expression for the number of users after $3$ weeks, we get\n\n$$\n125\\cdot (1.4)^{3}=343.\n$$\n\nHence, after $3$ weeks, there will be $343$ users."] [17592186636447 :question/problem "A youth soccer club started with $20$ members. The number of members then increases by $40\\%$ every year. How many members will the club have after $4$ years?"] [17592186636447 :question/worked-solution "The soccer club started with $20$ members, and this number increases by $40\\%$ each year.\n\n$40\\%$ as a decimal is $0.4.$ Therefore, an increase of $40\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.4=1.4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ year, the club will have $20\\cdot (1.4)$ members.\n\n- After $2$ years, the club will have $20\\cdot (1.4)^{2}$ members.\n\n- After $3$ years, the club will have $20\\cdot (1.4)^{3}$ members.\n\n- After $4$ years, the club will have $20\\cdot (1.4)^{4}$ members\n\nEvaluating the expression for the number of members after $4$ years, we get\n\n$$\n20\\cdot (1.4)^{4}≈77.\n$$\n\nWe round to the nearest whole number because we are talking about people."] [17592186636471 :question/problem "Consider the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhat are the dimensions of ${B}^{T}?$"] [17592186636502 :question/problem "Find ${S}^{T}$ given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle S=\\begin{bmatrix}a & -b & c\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186636518 :question/problem "Mia invests $\\$3\\,500$ in a savings account. The amount of money in the account increases by $13\\%$ each year. How much money will Mia have in her account in $8$ years? Round your answer to the nearest dollar."] [17592186636518 :question/worked-solution "The savings account starts at $\\$3\\,500$, and this amount increases by $13\\%$ each year.\n\n$13\\%$ as a decimal is $0.13.$ Therefore, an increase of $13\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.13=1.13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ year, Mia will have $3\\,500\\cdot (1.13)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, Mia will have $3\\,500\\cdot (1.13)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, Mia will have $3\\,500\\cdot (1.13)^{3}$ dollars.\n\n$\\,⋮$\n\n- After $8$ years, Mia will have $3\\,500\\cdot (1.13)^{8}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the expression for the amount of money Mia will have after $8$ years, we get\n\n$$\n3\\,500\\cdot (1.13)^{8}≈9\\,305\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest dollar.\n\nHence, after $8$ years, Mia will have approximately $\\$9\\,305.$"] [17592186636532 :question/problem "A particular culture initially has $256$ bacteria. If the population grows by $75\\%$ each hour, how many bacteria will there be in four hours?\n\nIn $4$ hours, there will be\n{{field-1}}\nbacteria in the culture."] [17592186636532 :question/worked-solution "The culture starts with $256$ bacteria, and this amount increases by $75\\%$ each hour.\n\n$75\\%$ as a decimal is $0.75.$ Therefore, an increase of $75\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.75=1.75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ hour, the culture has $256\\cdot (1.75)$ bacteria.\n\n- After $2$ hours, the culture will have $256\\cdot (1.75)^{2}$ bacteria.\n\n- After $3$ hours, the culture will have $256\\cdot (1.75)^{3}$ bacteria.\n\n- After $4$ hours, the culture will have $256\\cdot (1.75)^{4}$ bacteria.\n\nEvaluating the expression for the population after $4$ hours, we get\n\n$$\n256\\cdot (1.75)^{4}=2\\,401.\n$$\n\nHence, after $4$ hours, there will be $2\\,401$ bacteria in the culture."] [17592186636541 :question/problem "Peter invests $\\$2\\,000$ in a savings account. The amount in the account increases by $11\\%$ each year. How much money will Peter have from this investment after $6$ years? Round your answer to the nearest dollar."] [17592186636541 :question/worked-solution "The savings account starts at $\\$2\\,000$, and this amount increases by $11\\%$ each year.\n\n$11\\%$ as a decimal is $0.11.$ Therefore, an increase of $11\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.11=1.11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ year, Peter will have $2\\,000\\cdot (1.11)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, Peter will have $2\\,000\\cdot (1.11)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, Peter will have $2\\,000\\cdot (1.11)^{3}$ dollars.\n\n$\\,⋮$\n\n- After $6$ years, Peter will have $2\\,000\\cdot (1.11)^{6}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the expression for the amount of money Peter will have after $6$ years, we get:\n\n$$\n2\\,000\\cdot (1.11)^{6}≈3\\,741\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer value. \n\nHence, after $6$ years, Peter will have approximately $\\$3\\,741.$"] [17592186637235 :question/problem "There are three groups of music lovers in a particular town: those who prefer jazz, those who prefer classical music, and those who prefer rock music. A study has shown that, each year\n\n- from the people that prefer jazz, $10\\%$ change to classical music and $15\\%$ change to rock music,\n\n- from the people that prefer classical music, $5\\%$ change to jazz and $7\\%$ change to rock music,\n\n- from the people that prefer rock music, $15\\%$ change to jazz and $12\\%$ change to classical music.\n\nThe stochastic matrix that describes this situation is given below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.75 & 0.05 & a \\\\ 0.1 & b & 0.12 \\\\ c & 0.07 & 0.73\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186637235 :question/worked-solution "We are given the following situation for each year:\n\n- From the people that prefer jazz, $10\\%$ change to classical music and $15\\%$ change to rock music. This implies that $75\\%$ of them continue to prefer jazz.\n\n- From people that prefer classical music, $5\\%$ change to jazz and $7\\%$ change to rock music. This implies that $88\\%$ of them continue to prefer classical music.\n\n- From people that prefer rock music, $15\\%$ change to jazz and $12\\%$ change to classical music. This implies that $73\\%$ of them continue to prefer rock music.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Jazz</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Classics</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Rock</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Jazz</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.75$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.05$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Classics</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.88$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Rock</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.07$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.73$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nWe can write the corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.75 & 0.05 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.88 & 0.12 \\\\ 0.15 & 0.07 & 0.73\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0.15+0.88+0.15=1.18.\n$$"] [17592186637256 :question/problem "One month, the earnings of a shoe store were $\\$5\\,000.$ For all subsequent months, the store's earnings $y(t),$ in dollars, after $t$ months is given by the formula:\n\n$$\ny=5\\,000\\cdot (\\text{___})^{t}\n$$\n\nIf the store's earnings increase by $4\\%$ each month, then what is the missing value in the expression for $y?$"] [17592186637256 :question/worked-solution "The earnings $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $4\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=\\$5\\,000$ gives the store's initial earnings.\n\n- $r=0.04$ is the growth rate ($4\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in months.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\,000\\cdot (1+0.04)^{t} \\\\  & =5\\,000\\cdot (1.04)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $1.04.$"] [17592186637265 :question/problem "There are three groups of popcorn lovers among the customers of a certain cinema: those who prefer popcorn with salt, those who prefer popcorn with cheese, and those who prefer popcorn with caramel. A study has shown that each month\n\n- from the customers that prefer popcorn with salt, $20\\%$ change to cheese flavor, and $10\\%$ change to caramel,\n\n- from the customers that prefer popcorn with cheese, $10\\%$ change to caramel flavor and $40\\%$ change to salt,\n\n- from the customers that prefer popcorn with caramel, $35\\%$ change to salt and $15\\%$ change to cheese.\n\nThe stochastic matrix that describes this situation is given below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.7 & 0.4 & a \\\\ 0.2 & b & 0.15 \\\\ c & 0.1 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186637265 :question/worked-solution "We are given the following situation for each month:\n\n- From the customers that prefer popcorn with salt, $20\\%$ change to cheese, and $10\\%$ change to caramel. This implies that $70\\%$ of them continue to prefer popcorn with salt.\n\n- From the customers that prefer popcorn with cheese, $10\\%$ change to caramel and $40\\%$ change to salt. This implies that $50\\%$ of them continue to prefer popcorn with cheese.\n\n- From the customers that prefer popcorn with caramel, $35\\%$ change to salt and $15\\%$ change to cheese. This implies that $50\\%$ of them continue to prefer popcorn with caramel.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Salt</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Cheese</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Caramel</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Salt</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Cheese</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Caramel</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nWe can write the corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.7 & 0.4 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.5 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0.35+0.5+0.1=0.95.\n$$"] [17592186637271 :question/problem "A certain company produces $2$ types of oil: vegetable oil and olive oil. The following table describes how the number of consumers changes between both types of oil each month:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"2\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Vegetable Oil</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Olive Oil</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Vegetable Oil</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Olive Oil</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.65$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nThat is, $60\\%$ of vegetable oil customers switch to olive oil each month, and $35\\%$ of olive oil customers switch to vegetable oil.\n\nCurrently, $700$ customers use vegetable oil while $300$ customers use olive oil. How many customers will use olive oil in one month? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change."] [17592186637271 :question/worked-solution "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.65\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of oil is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}700 \\\\ 300\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\begin{bmatrix}700 \\\\ 300\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.65\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.385 \\\\ 0.615\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one month, $61.5\\%$ of customers will use olive oil. Computing $61.5\\%$ of $1\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\cdot 0.615=615.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $615$ customers will use olive oil in one month."] [17592186637276 :question/problem "At the time of its release, a new game already had 40 downloads. At the end of each day after release, the number of downloads increased by $150\\%$ of the number of downloads at the end of the previous day.\n\nAccording to this model, how many downloads are there estimated to be after $2$ days?"] [17592186637276 :question/worked-solution "The number of downloads $D(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $150\\%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=40$ is the total number of downloads initially\n\n- $r=1.5$ is the growth rate ($150\\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in days) after the release\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nD=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $D(t)$ is\n\n$$\nD=40\\cdot (1+1.5)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nD(t)=40\\cdot (2.5)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of downloads after $2$ days, we evaluate the above for $t=2:$\n\n$$\nD(2)=40\\cdot (2.5)^{2}=40\\cdot 6.25=250\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of downloads after $2$ days is $250.$"] [17592186637282 :question/problem "Initially, an electronic store earned $\\$7\\,500$ per month. After an effective marketing campaign, the monthly earnings increased by $12\\%$ each month. \n\nThe store's monthly earnings $y(t),$ in dollars, after $t$ months is given by the formula\n\n$$\ny(t)=7\\,500\\cdot (\\text{___})^{t}\\,.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing value in the expression for $y?$"] [17592186637282 :question/worked-solution "The monthly earnings $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $12\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=7\\,500$ dollars was the initial revenue of the store.\n\n- $r=0.12$ is the growth rate ($12\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in months.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7\\,500\\cdot (1+0.12)^{t} \\\\  & =7\\,500\\cdot (1.12)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $1.12.$"] [17592186637315 :question/problem "A charity event began with $20$ donations. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each day after the event began, the number of donations increased by $200\\%$ of the number of donations at the end of the previous day.\n\nAccording to this model, how many donations are there estimated to be after $2$ days?"] [17592186637315 :question/worked-solution "The number of donations $D(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $200\\%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=20$ is the total number of donations initially\n\n- $r=2.0$ is the growth rate ($200\\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in days) after the event began\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nD=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $D(t)$ is\n\n$$\nD=20\\cdot (1+2.0)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nD(t)=20\\cdot (3)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of donations after $2$ days, we evaluate the above for $t=2:$\n\n$$\nD(2)=20\\cdot (3)^{2}=20\\cdot 9=180\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of donations after $2$ days is $180.$"] [17592186637324 :question/problem "There are $80$ mice on a farm, and the number of mice increases by $18\\%$ each month. What function represents the number of mice $y(t)$ that will be on the farm after $t$ months?"] [17592186637324 :question/worked-solution "The number of mice $y(t)$ will grow exponentially with a growth rate of $18\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=80$ is the initial number of mice on the farm.\n\n- $b=0.18$ is the growth rate ($18\\%$ expressed as a percent).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in months.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =80\\cdot (1+0.18)^{t} \\\\  & =80\\cdot {1.18}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186637329 :question/problem "A certain company produces three types of rice: Type I, Type II, and Type III. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between the three types of rice each year:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Type I</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Type II</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Type III</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Type I</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Type II</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Type III</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nFor example, $10\\%$ of Type I rice customers switch to Type II each year, and $50\\%$ of Type III customers switch to Type I.\n\nCurrently, $500\\,000$ customers buy Type I rice, $300\\,000$ customers buy Type II rice, and $200\\,000$ customers buy Type III  rice. How many customers will purchase Type II rice in one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change."] [17592186637329 :question/worked-solution "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.7 & 0.3 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of rice is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}500\\,000 \\\\ 300\\,000 \\\\ 200\\,000\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000\\,000}\\begin{bmatrix}500\\,000 \\\\ 300\\,000 \\\\ 200\\,000\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 \\\\ 0.3 \\\\ 0.2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.7 & 0.3 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 \\\\ 0.3 \\\\ 0.2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.54 \\\\ 0.21 \\\\ 0.25\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $21\\%$ of customers will buy Type II rice. Computing $21\\%$ of $1\\,000\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.21=210\\,000.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $210\\,000$ customers will buy Type II rice in one year."] [17592186638235 :question/worked-solution "The answer choices require interpretation as a percent, and so we use the growth rate formula.\n\nThe given expression is an exponential growth expression of the form $V(t)=a\\cdot (1+r)^{t}$, where:\n\n- $a=1\\,000\\,000$ represents the number of visitors in $2016$\n\n- $1+r=1.5,$ where $r$ is the growth rate\n\n- $t=$ the time since the year $2016$\n\nSolving for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n1+r=1.5\\,⟹\\,r=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, $0.5$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $50\\%.$ Therefore, the number of visitors increased by $50\\%$ per year."] [17592186638236 :question/problem "The migration table below describes the movement of the population between cities, suburbs, and towns every year in a specific state.  \n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Cities</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Suburbs</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Towns</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Cities</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Suburbs</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Towns</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nFor example, $10\\%$ of those living in towns move to the suburbs each year.\n\nAssuming that the state's population is $10$ million and remains constant, how many people will eventually live in towns if the given probabilities remain constant over many years?"] [17592186638245 :question/worked-solution "The answer choices require interpretation as a percent, and so we use the growth rate formula.\n\nThe given expression is an exponential growth expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot (1+r)^{t}$, where:\n\n- $a=800$ represents the initial population of deer in the wildlife refuge\n\n- $1+r=1.14,$ where $r$ is the growth rate\n\n- $t$ is the number of years since $2000$\n\nSolving for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n1+r=1.14\\,⟹\\,r=0.14.\n$$\n\nNow, $0.14$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $14\\%.$ Therefore, the deer population grows at a rate of $14\\%$ per year."] [17592186638255 :question/problem "The price of a gallon of gasoline is $\\$3.$ Some economists estimate that its price will rise by $1\\%$ weekly.  Complete the following equation that we should solve to determine how many weeks it will take for the price to increase to $\\$3.5.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{t}=3.5$"] [17592186638255 :question/worked-solution "The price $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $1\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=3$ is the initial price in dollars.\n\n- $r=0.01$ is the growth rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in weeks, since the current moment.\n\nSo, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =3\\cdot (1+0.01)^{t} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (1.01)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the time it takes for the price to increase to $\\$3.5,$ we would need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =3.5 \\\\ 3\\cdot (1.01)^{t} & =3.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186638286 :question/problem "Andrew deposits $\\$2\\,000$ into a savings account that earns $15\\%$ interest per year. Complete the following equation that we should solve to determine how many years it will take for the balance in the account to increase to $\\$2\\,400.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\,(${{field-2}}$)^{t}=2\\,400$"] [17592186638286 :question/worked-solution "The investment $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $15\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=2\\,000$ is the initial investment, in dollars.\n\n- $r=0.15$ is the growth rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in years, since Andrew deposited $\\$2\\,000$ into a savings account.\n\nSo, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =2\\,000\\cdot (1+0.15)^{t} \\\\  & =2\\,000\\cdot (1.15)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the time it takes for the balance in the account to increase to $\\$2\\,400,$ we would need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =2\\,400 \\\\ 2\\,000\\cdot (1.15)^{t} & =2\\,400.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186638295 :question/problem "A town with an initial population of $25\\,000$ grows at a rate of $2.5\\%$ per year. What does the expression  $25\\,000\\cdot (1.025)^{10}$ mean in this context?"] [17592186638299 :question/worked-solution "The answer choices require interpretation as a percent, and so we use the growth rate formula.\n\nThe given expression is an exponential growth expression of the form $A(t)=a\\cdot (1+r)^{t}$, where:\n\n- $a=5$ represents the initial number of people with allergies\n\n- $1+r=1.25,$ where $r$ is the growth rate\n\n- $t=$ the time since the first cases were reported\n\nSolving for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n1+r=1.25\\,⟹\\,r=0.25.\n$$\n\nNow, $0.25$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $25\\%.$ Therefore, the number of people who got allergies increased by $25\\%$ every week."] [17592186638309 :question/problem "There were initially $160$ mice in a field. Due to the decrease in predators, the number of mice in the field increases by $17\\%$ every month. Complete the following equation that we should solve to determine how many months it will take for the mouse population to increase to $600.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{t}=600$"] [17592186638309 :question/worked-solution "The mouse population $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $17\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=160$ is the initial number of mice.\n\n- $r=0.17$ is the growth rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in months, since the beginning of the observation.\n\nSo, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =160\\cdot (1+0.17)^{t} \\\\  & =160\\cdot (1.17)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the time it will take for the mouse population to increase to $600,$ we would need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =600 \\\\ 160\\cdot (1.17)^{t} & =600.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186639317 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle U=\\begin{bmatrix}-3v & -7 \\\\ 8 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\text{det}(U)=-16$, what is the value of $v?$"] [17592186639327 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle U=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -6 \\\\ -4k & 8k+2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\text{det}(U)=-6$, what is the value of $k?$"] [17592186639331 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle U=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 7 \\\\ 7k & 7k-5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\text{det}(U)=-89$, what is the value of $k?$"] [17592186640768 :question/problem "The population of elephants in a park decreases at a rate of $20\\%$ annually. If there are $760$ elephants in the park currently, approximately how many elephants will there be after $4$ years?"] [17592186640768 :question/worked-solution "The number $20\\%$ (expressed as a decimal) is equal to $0.2.$ Therefore, a decrease of $20\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of:\n\n$$\n1-0.2=0.8\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ year, the population will be $760\\cdot (0.8).$\n\n- After $2$ years, the elephant population will be approximately $760\\cdot (0.8)^{2}.$\n\n- After $3$ years, the elephant population will be approximately $760\\cdot (0.8)^{3}.$\n\n- After $4$ years, the elephant population will be approximately $760\\cdot (0.8)^{4}.$\n\nEvaluating the last expression gives\n\n$$\n760\\cdot (0.8)^{4}≈311.\n$$\n\nSo there will be approximately $311$ elephants remaining after $4$ years."] [17592186641622 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage error}=\\frac{|\\text{estimate}-\\text{exact value}|}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100\\%.\n$$\n\nWe have the following information:\n\n- $\\text{estimate}=54$ tons\n\n- $\\text{exact value}=54-9=45$ tons\n\nSubstituting these values into our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{percentage error} & =\\frac{|54-45|}{45}\\times 100\\% \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{45}\\times 100\\% \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\times 100\\% \\\\  & =0.2\\times 100\\% \\\\  & =20\\%.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the percentage error is $20\\%.$"] [17592186641635 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage change}=\\frac{|\\text{new value}-\\text{original value}|}{\\text{original value}}\\times 100\\%.\n$$\n\nLet $x$ be the original value. Then, we have the following information:\n\n- $\\text{new value}=2.1x$\n\n- $\\text{original value}=x$\n\nSubstituting these values into our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{percentage change} & =\\frac{|2.1x-x|}{x}\\times 100\\% \\\\  & =\\frac{|1.1x|}{x}\\times 100\\% \\\\  & =\\frac{1.1x}{x}\\times 100\\% \\\\  & =\\frac{1.1\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}}\\times 100\\% \\\\  & =1.1\\times 100\\% \\\\  & =110\\%.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smaller number is increased by $110\\%.$"] [17592186641636 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage change}=\\frac{|\\text{new value}-\\text{original value}|}{\\text{original value}}\\times 100\\%.\n$$\n\nWe have the following information:\n\n- $\\text{new value}=450$ minutes\n\n- $\\text{original value}=500$ minutes\n\nSubstituting these values into our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{percentage change} & =\\frac{|450-500|}{500}\\times 100\\% \\\\  & =\\frac{|-50|}{500}\\times 100\\% \\\\  & =\\frac{50}{500}\\times 100\\% \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\times 100\\% \\\\  & =0.1\\times 100\\% \\\\  & =10\\%.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the percentage decrease is $10\\%.$"] [17592186641651 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage change}=\\frac{|\\text{new value}-\\text{original value}|}{\\text{original value}}\\times 100\\%.\n$$\n\nWe have the following information:\n\n- $\\text{new value}=46$ kilograms\n\n- $\\text{original value}=50$ kilograms\n\nSubstituting these values into our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{percentage change} & =\\frac{|46-50|}{50}\\times 100\\% \\\\  & =\\frac{|-4|}{50}\\times 100\\% \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{50}\\times 100\\% \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{25}\\times 100\\% \\\\  & =0.08\\times 100\\% \\\\  & =8\\%.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the percentage decrease is $8\\%.$"] [17592186641656 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage error}=\\frac{|\\text{estimate}-\\text{exact value}|}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100\\%.\n$$\n\nWe have the following information:\n\n- $\\text{estimate}=5,400$ tickets\n\n- $\\text{exact value}=5,000$ tickets\n\nSubstituting these values into our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{percentage error} & =\\frac{|5,400-5,000|}{5,000}\\times 100\\% \\\\  & =\\frac{400}{5,000}\\times 100\\% \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{25}\\times 100\\% \\\\  & =0.08\\times 100\\% \\\\  & =8\\%.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the percentage error is $8\\%.$"] [17592186644084 :question/problem "A forest area is initially $1.5$ square kilometers. If the area increases by $10\\%$ every year due to reforestation efforts, what will the area of the forest be in $12$ years? Round your answer to the nearest tenth of a square kilometer.\n\nTo the nearest tenth of a square kilometer, the forest area will be\n{{field-1}}\nsquare kilometers."] [17592186644084 :question/worked-solution "The forest area starts at $1.5$ square kilometers, and this amount increases by $10\\%$ each year.\n\n$10\\%$ as a decimal is $0.1.$ Therefore, an increase of $10\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.1=1.1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ year, the forest area is $1.5\\cdot (1.1)$ square kilometers.\n\n- After $2$ years, the forest area is $1.5\\cdot (1.1)^{2}$ square kilometers.\n\n- After $3$ years, the forest area is $1.5\\cdot (1.1)^{3}$ square kilometers.\n\n$\\,⋮$\n\n- After $12$ years, the forest area is $1.5\\cdot (1.1)^{12}$ square kilometers.\n\nEvaluating the expression for the area of the forest after $12$ years, we get\n\n$$\n1.5\\cdot (1.1)^{12}≈4.7\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth of a square kilometer.\n\nHence, after $12$ years and to the nearest tenth of a square kilometer, the forest area will be $4.7$ square kilometers."] [17592186644154 :question/problem "The salary of a certain college football coach had increased by $12\\%$ per year since $2020$ when his salary was $\\$65\\,000.$ If this trend continues, what will be the coach's salary in $2027?$ Round the answer to the nearest integer value."] [17592186644154 :question/worked-solution "The coach's salary was $\\$65\\,000$ in $2020$, and it has been increasing by $12\\%$ per year since then. \n\n$12\\%$ as a decimal is $0.12.$ Therefore, an increase of $12\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.12=1.12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ year, the coach's salary will be $65\\,000\\cdot (1.12)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, the coach's salary will be $65\\,000\\cdot (1.12)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, the coach's salary will be $65\\,000\\cdot (1.12)^{3}$ dollars.\n\n$\\,⋮$\n\n- After $7$ years, the coach's salary will be $65\\,000\\cdot (1.12)^{7}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the expression for the coach's salary after $7$ years, we get:\n\n$$\n65\\,000\\cdot (1.12)^{7}≈143\\,694\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nHence, after $7$ years, the coach's salary will be approximately $\\$143\\,694.$"] [17592186644993 :question/problem "A painter has to paint a wall that measures $256$ square feet. He can reduce unpainted wall area by $25\\%$ every hour. How long will it take until there are only $81$ square feet of wall left to paint?"] [17592186644993 :question/worked-solution "The area of the unpainted surface $y(t)$ decays exponentially with a decay rate of $25\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=256$ is the initial amount of unpainted surface.\n\n- $r=0.25$ is the decay rate ($25\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in hours.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=256\\cdot (1-0.25)^{t}=256\\cdot {(0.75)}^{t}.\n$$\n\nSince $0.75=\\frac{3}{4},$ this is equivalent to\n\n$$\ny(t)=256\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe want to know how many hours it takes for the unpainted surface to decrease to $81$ square feet, and so we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n256\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{t}=81.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}256\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{t} & =81 \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{t} & =\\frac{81}{256} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{t} & =\\frac{{3}^{4}}{{4}^{4}} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{t} & ={\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{4} \\\\ t & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, it takes $4$ hours for the unpainted surface area to reduce to $81$ square feet."] [17592186645033 :question/problem "An investment loses $9\\%$ of its value every month. If the investment is currently valued at $\\$750$, what equation should be solved to determine the number of months $t$ it will take for the value of the investment to reduce to $\\$240?$"] [17592186645033 :question/worked-solution "The value of the investment $y(t)$ decreases exponentially with a decay rate of $9\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=750$ is the value of the investment.\n\n- $r=0.09$ is the decay rate ($9\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in months.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $v$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=750\\cdot (1-0.09)^{t}=750\\cdot (0.91)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSince we want to know how many months it takes for the value of the investment to decrease to $\\$240$, we need to solve the equation $y(t)=240.$ This gives\n\n$$\n750\\cdot (0.91)^{t}=240.\n$$"] [17592186645042 :question/problem "In an antivirus file analysis, the number of files to be analyzed decreases by $35\\%$ every second since the analysis started. If the initial number of files was $2\\,000,$ which equation gives the number of seconds it will take for the number of files to be analyzed to decrease to $5?$"] [17592186645042 :question/worked-solution "The number of files to be analyzed $f(t)$ decreases exponentially with a decay rate of $35\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is:\n\n$$\nf(t)=a(1-r)^{t}\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=2\\,000$ is the initial number of files to be analyzed.\n\n- $r=0.35$ is the decay rate ($35\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in seconds.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $f$ is:\n\n$$\nf(t)=2\\,000\\cdot (1-0.35)^{t}=2\\,000\\cdot (0.65)^{t}\n$$\n\nSince we want to know how many seconds it takes to decrease to $5$ files, we need to solve the equation $f(t)=5.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n2\\,000\\cdot (0.65)^{t}=5.\n$$"] [17592186645057 :question/problem "The number of elephants in a herd reduces by $10\\%$ every year. Currently, there are $729$ elephants in the herd. How many elephants were in the herd $3$ years ago?"] [17592186645057 :question/worked-solution "The number of elephants $y(t)$ decays exponentially with a decay rate of $10\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a$ is the initial number of elephants (which is what we're trying to find).\n\n- $r=0.10$ is the decay rate ($10\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a\\cdot (1-0.10)^{t}=a\\cdot (0.90)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe know that there were $729$ elephants after $3$ years. Therefore,\n\n$$\na\\cdot (0.90)^{3}=729.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{729}{(0.90)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{729}{0.729} \\\\  & =1\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186645092 :question/problem "Due to disease, the number of trees in a national park decreases by $40\\%$ every decade. Currently, there are $9\\,720$ trees. How many trees were in the park $5$ decades ago?"] [17592186645092 :question/worked-solution "The number of trees $y(t)$ decays exponentially with a decay rate of $40\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a$ is the initial number of trees (which is what we're trying to find).\n\n- $r=0.4$ is the decay rate ($40\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in decades.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a\\cdot (1-0.4)^{t}=a\\cdot (0.6)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the number of trees was $9\\,720$ after $5$ decades. Therefore,\n\n$$\na\\cdot (0.6)^{5}=9\\,720.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{9\\,720}{(0.60)^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\,720}{0.07776} \\\\  & =125\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186645718 :question/problem "A student is randomly answering questions in a $16$-question math test. It is known that the student has a $25\\%$ probability of correctly answering each question. What is the probability that the student answers at least $3$ questions correctly? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186645718 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of questions that the student answers correctly.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=16$ questions\n\n- the probability of answering each question correctly is $p=25\\%=0.25$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(16,0.25).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{x})(0.25)^{x}(1-0.25)^{16-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{x})(0.25)^{x}(0.75)^{16-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the student answers at least $3$ questions correctly. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 3).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 3)=P(X=3)+P(X=4)+…+P(X=16)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{0})(0.25)^{0}(0.75)^{16}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{1})(0.25)^{1}(0.75)^{15}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{2})(0.25)^{2}(0.75)^{14}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.75)^{16}+16(0.25)^{1}(0.75)^{15}+120(0.25)^{2}(0.75)^{14}] \\\\  & =1-0.197\\,111 \\\\  & =0.803\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186646277 :question/problem "When a science blog was first posted, it had $30$ views. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each day after posting, the number of views increased by $110\\%$ of the number of views at the end of the previous day.\n\nWhich of the following equations best represents this model, where $V$ is the estimated number of views $t$ days after the blog was posted, and $t\\le 6?$"] [17592186646277 :question/worked-solution "The number of views $V(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $110\\%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=30$ is the total number of views initially\n\n- $r=1.1$ is the growth rate ($110\\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in days) after posting\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nV=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $V(t)$ is\n\n$$\nV=30\\cdot (1+1.1)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nV(t)=30\\cdot (2.1)^{t}.\n$$"] [17592186646294 :question/problem "Find ${M}^{T}$ given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle M=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186646297 :question/problem "In a tropical forest, there are $150$ Malachite Kingfishers. Every year, the number of Kingfishers increases by $30\\%.$ What function represents the number of Kingfishers $y(t)$ after $t$ years?"] [17592186646297 :question/worked-solution "The number of Kingfishers $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $30\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=150$ is the initial number of Kingfishers.\n\n- $b=0.3$ is the growth rate ($30\\%$ expressed as a percent).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =150\\cdot (1+0.3)^{t} \\\\  & =150\\cdot {1.3}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186646343 :question/problem "When a music video was first uploaded, it had $80$ views. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each hour after upload, the number of views increased by $200\\%$ of the number of views at the end of the previous hour.\n\nWhich of the following equations best represents this model, where $V$ is the estimated number of views $t$ hours after the video was uploaded, and $t\\le 4?$"] [17592186646343 :question/worked-solution "The number of views $V(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $200\\%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=80$ is the total number of views initially\n\n- $r=2.0$ is the growth rate ($200\\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in hours) after upload\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nV=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $V(t)$ is\n\n$$\nV=80\\cdot (1+2.0)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nV(t)=80\\cdot (3)^{t}.\n$$"] [17592186647093 :question/problem "Find ${R}^{T}$ given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle R=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186648615 :question/problem "A survey of Bulgarian citizens showed that $38\\%$ of all males smoke. Overall, $27.3\\%$ of the population smoke, and $49\\%$ of the population are male. If a smoker from this population is selected at random, what is the probability of them being male? Express your answer as a percentage to the nearest percent."] [17592186648615 :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be the event that a randomly selected person smokes, and let $M$ be the event that they are male. Then, we require $P(M|S),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(M|S)=\\frac{P(S|M)P(M)}{P(S)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- $P(S|M)=0.38,$ since $38\\%$ of all males smoke.\n\n- $P(M)=0.49,$ since $49\\%$ of the population are male.\n\n- $P(S)=0.273,$ since $27\\%$ of the population smoke.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M|S) & =\\frac{P(S|M)P(M)}{P(S)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.38)(0.49)}{0.273} \\\\  & ≈68\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent."] [17592186648679 :question/problem "The lifetime of bulbs produced by a particular company is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu$ hours and a standard deviation of $800$ hours. Additionally, $15\\%$ of the bulbs have a lifetime lower than $5\\,200$ hours. Find the percentage of bulbs with a lifetime greater than $7\\,000$ hours.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8643\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8849\\,$ | $\\,0.8869\\,$ | $\\,0.8888\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9032\\,$ | $\\,0.9049\\,$ | $\\,0.9066\\,$ |"] [17592186648679 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the lifetime of a bulb. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{800}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>7\\,000).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<5\\,200)=0.15.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5\\,200) & =0.15 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{5\\,200-\\mu }{800}) & =0.15 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{5\\,200-\\mu }{800}\\right) & =0.15 \\\\ \\frac{5\\,200-\\mu }{800} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.15)≈-1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\,200-\\mu }{800} & =-1.0364 \\\\ 5\\,200-\\mu  & =-829.12 \\\\ \\mu  & =6\\,029.12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X>7\\,000)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>7\\,000) & =P(Z>\\frac{7\\,000-6\\,029.12}{800}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.21) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.21) \\\\  & =1-0.8869 \\\\  & ≈11.3\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $11.3\\%$ of the bulbs have a lifetime greater than $7\\,000$ hours."] [17592186649167 :question/problem "The lifetime of a lithium-ion battery produced by a particular company is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu$ years and a standard deviation of $0.5$ years. Additionally, $5\\%$ of batteries last less than $2.18$ years. Find the percentage of batteries that last over $3.5$ years, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ |"] [17592186649167 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the lifetime of a randomly selected battery. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.5}^{2}),$ and we want to find the $P(X>3.5).$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<2.18)=0.05.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2.18) & =0.05 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{2.18-\\mu }{0.5}) & =0.05 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{2.18-\\mu }{0.5}\\right) & =0.05 \\\\ \\frac{2.18-\\mu }{0.5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.05)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.05)≈-1.6449.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2.18-\\mu }{0.5} & =-1.6449 \\\\ 2.18-\\mu  & =-0.82245 \\\\ \\mu  & =3.00245.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X>3.5)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3.5) & =1-P(X\\le 3.5) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le \\frac{3.5-3.00245}{0.5}) \\\\  & ≈1-P(Z\\le 1) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1) \\\\  & =1-0.8413 \\\\  & ≈15.9\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $15.9\\%$ of batteries last more than $3.5$ years."] [17592186649215 :question/problem "A smartphone’s battery life is normally distributed with a mean of $20$ hours and an unknown standard deviation $\\sigma .$ Tests show that $25\\%$ of phones run out of charge in less than $17$ hours. Assuming a normal model, find the standard deviation, **rounded to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $\\,p\\,$ | $0.75$ | $0.8$ | $0.85$ | $0.9$ | $0.95$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma ≈$\n{{field-1}}$\\text{h}$"] [17592186649815 :question/problem "A TV store is offering a discount of $20\\%$ on all purchases. What is $20\\%$ expressed as a fraction?"] [17592186649815 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the number in front of the percent sign $(20)$ as a fraction with denominator $100:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{20}{100}\n$$\n\nWe now need to simplify this fraction. Dividing the numerator and denominator by $10,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{20}{100}=\\frac{20÷10}{100÷10}=\\frac{2}{10}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{10}=\\frac{2÷2}{10÷2}=\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $20\\%=\\frac{1}{5}.$"] [17592186649823 :question/worked-solution "First, we write $\\frac{6}{5}$ as an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{5}=\\frac{6\\times 20}{5\\times 20}=\\frac{120}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{120}{100}=120\\%\n$$"] [17592186649825 :question/problem "James bought a car for $\\$9,900$ before tax. If the sales tax was $7\\%,$ then what was James's total bill after tax?"] [17592186649825 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the amount of tax that James must pay. Then, we will add that amount to the price of the car.\n\nThe tax rate is $7\\%$ of the price of the car, and the price of the car is $\\$9,900.$ So, to calculate how much tax James must pay, we need to find $7\\%$ of $\\$9,900.$\n\nTo do this, we first write $7\\%$ as a decimal:\n\n$$\n7\\%=7÷100=0.07\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $9,900:$\n\n$$\n0.07\\times 9,900=693\n$$\n\nSo James must pay $\\$693$ in tax. We add this amount to the price of the car:\n\n$$\n9,900+693=10,593\n$$\n\nTherefore, James's total bill after tax is $\\$10,593.$"] [17592186649830 :question/worked-solution "First, we write $\\frac{9}{10}$ as an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{10}=\\frac{9\\times 10}{10\\times 10}=\\frac{90}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{90}{100}=90\\%\n$$"] [17592186649836 :question/problem "Frank's Fish is having a sale in which a $25\\%$ markdown is applied to all items in the store. If Alec buys some monkfish for $\\$12$ before the markdown is applied, what is Alec's total bill after the markdown?"] [17592186649836 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the amount of the markdown. Then, we will subtract that amount from the price of the monkfish.\n\nThe markdown is $25\\%$ of the price of the monkfish, and the price of the monkfish is $\\$12.$ So, to calculate the amount of the markdown, we need to find $25\\%$ of $\\$12.$\n\nTo do this, we first write $25\\%$ as a decimal:\n\n$$\n25\\%=25÷100=0.25\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $12:$\n\n$$\n0.25\\times 12=3\n$$\n\nSo the amount of the markdown is $\\$3.$ We subtract this amount from the price of the monkfish:\n\n$$\n12-3=9\n$$\n\nTherefore, Alec's total bill after the markdown is $\\$9.$"] [17592186649839 :question/worked-solution "First, we write $\\frac{7}{20}$ as an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{20}=\\frac{7\\times 5}{20\\times 5}=\\frac{35}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{35}{100}=35\\%\n$$"] [17592186649845 :question/problem "Lucy has a voucher in which a $15\\%$ discount is applied to a meal in a restaurant. If Lucy's bill is $\\$180$ before the discount is applied, how much is the discount?"] [17592186649845 :question/worked-solution "There is a $15\\%$ discount on the price of the meal. Here, the price of the meal is $\\$180.$ So to calculate the amount of the discount, we need to find $15\\%$ of $\\$180.$\n\nTo do this, we first write $15\\%$ as a decimal:\n\n$$\n15\\%=15÷100=0.15\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $180:$\n\n$$\n0.15\\times 180=27\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of the discount is $\\$27.$"] [17592186649849 :question/problem "Erol bought a calculator for $\\$25$ before tax. If the sales tax was $12\\%,$ then what was Erol's total bill after tax?"] [17592186649849 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the amount of tax that Erol must pay. Then, we will add that amount to the price of the calculator.\n\nThe tax rate is $12\\%$ of the price of the calculator, and the price of the calculator is $\\$25.$ So, to calculate how much tax Erol must pay, we need to find $12\\%$ of $\\$25.$\n\nTo do this, we first write $12\\%$ as a decimal:\n\n$$\n12\\%=12÷100=0.12\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $25:$\n\n$$\n0.12\\times 25=3\n$$\n\nSo Erol must pay $\\$3$ in tax. We add this amount to the price of the calculator:\n\n$$\n25+3=28\n$$\n\nTherefore, Erol's total bill after tax is $\\$28.$"] [17592186649850 :question/problem "Becky's Books is having a sale in which a $20\\%$ markdown is applied to all books. If a book costs $\\$15$ before the markdown is applied, how much is the markdown?"] [17592186649850 :question/worked-solution "There is a $20\\%$ markdown on the price of a book. Here, the price of a book is $\\$15.$ So to calculate the amount of the markdown, we need to find $20\\%$ of $\\$15.$\n\nTo do this, we first write $20\\%$ as a decimal:\n\n$$\n20\\%=20÷100=0.2\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $15:$\n\n$$\n0.2\\times 15=3\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of the markdown is $\\$3.$"] [17592186649851 :question/problem "Ross bought a television for $\\$375$ before tax. If the sales tax was $16\\%,$ then what was Ross's total bill after tax?"] [17592186649851 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the amount of tax that Ross must pay. Then, we will add that amount to the price of the television.\n\nThe tax rate is $16\\%$ of the price of the television, and the price of the television is $\\$375.$ So, to calculate how much tax Ross must pay, we need to find $16\\%$ of $\\$375.$\n\nTo do this, we first write $16\\%$ as a decimal:\n\n$$\n16\\%=16÷100=0.16\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $375:$\n\n$$\n0.16\\times 375=60\n$$\n\nSo Ross must pay $\\$60$ in tax. We add this amount to the price of the television:\n\n$$\n375+60=435\n$$\n\nTherefore, Ross's total bill after tax is $\\$435.$"] [17592186649852 :question/problem "Ralf plays baseball for his school's team. He hit $30\\%$ of his team's home runs. What fraction of his team's home runs did Ralf hit?"] [17592186649852 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the number in front of the percent sign ($30$) as a fraction with denominator $100:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{30}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $10:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{30}{100}=\\frac{30÷10}{100÷10}=\\frac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $30\\%=\\frac{3}{10}.$"] [17592186649853 :question/worked-solution "First, we write $\\frac{3}{20}$ as an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{20}=\\frac{3\\times 5}{20\\times 5}=\\frac{15}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{100}=15\\%\n$$"] [17592186649857 :question/problem "David bought a computer for $\\$575$ before tax. If the sales tax was $12\\%,$ then how much does he pay in tax?"] [17592186649857 :question/worked-solution "The tax rate is $12\\%$ of the price of the computer. In this case, the price of the computer is $\\$575.$ So, to calculate how much David pays in tax, we need to find $12\\%$ of $\\$575.$\n\nTo do this, we first write $12\\%$ as a decimal:\n\n$$\n12\\%=12÷100=0.12\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $575:$\n\n$$\n0.12\\times 575=69\n$$\n\nTherefore, David pays $\\$69$ in tax."] [17592186649859 :question/problem "Phoebe's Photography is having a sale in which a $9\\%$ markdown is applied to all items in the store. If Emilia buys a camera for $\\$400$ before the markdown is applied, what is Emilia's total bill after the markdown?"] [17592186649859 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the amount of the markdown. Then, we will subtract that amount from the price of the camera.\n\nThe markdown is $9\\%$ of the price of the camera, and the price of the camera is $\\$400.$ So, to calculate the amount of the markdown, we need to find $9\\%$ of $\\$400.$\n\nTo do this, we first write $9\\%$ as a decimal:\n\n$$\n9\\%=9÷100=0.09\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $400:$\n\n$$\n0.09\\times 400=36\n$$\n\nSo the amount of the markdown is $\\$36.$ We subtract this amount from the price of the camera:\n\n$$\n400-36=364\n$$\n\nTherefore, Emilia's total bill after the markdown is $\\$364.$"] [17592186649860 :question/problem "$65\\%=$"] [17592186649860 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the number in front of the percent sign ($65$) as a fraction with denominator $100$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{65}{100}\n$$\n\nWe now simplify fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{65}{100}=\\frac{65÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{13}{20}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $65\\%=\\frac{13}{20}.$"] [17592186649861 :question/problem "Claire's Ceramics is having a sale in which a $45\\%$ discount is applied to all bowls. If a bowl costs $\\$40$ before the discount is applied, how much is the discount?"] [17592186649861 :question/worked-solution "There is a $45\\%$ discount on the price of a bowl. Here, the price of a bowl is $\\$40.$ So to calculate the amount of the discount, we need to find $45\\%$ of $\\$40.$\n\nTo do this, we first write $45\\%$ as a decimal:\n\n$$\n45\\%=45÷100=0.45\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $40:$\n\n$$\n0.45\\times 40=18\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of the discount is $\\$18.$"] [17592186649867 :question/problem "$130\\%=$"] [17592186649867 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the number in front of the percent sign ($130$) as a fraction with denominator $100:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{130}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $10:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{130}{100}=\\frac{130÷10}{100÷10}=\\frac{13}{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $130\\%=\\frac{13}{10}.$"] [17592186649873 :question/problem "Terri's Toys is having a sale in which an $18\\%$ discount is applied to all items in store. If Annie buys a robot for $\\$50$ before the discount is applied, what is Annie's total bill after the discount?"] [17592186649873 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the amount of the discount. Then, we will subtract that amount from the price of the robot.\n\nThe discount is $18\\%$ of the price of the robot, and the price of the robot is $\\$50.$ So, to calculate the amount of the discount, we need to find $18\\%$ of $\\$50.$\n\nTo do this, we first write $18\\%$ as a decimal:\n\n$$\n18\\%=18÷100=0.18\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $50:$\n\n$$\n0.18\\times 50=9\n$$\n\nSo the amount of the discount is $\\$9.$ We subtract this amount from the price of the robot:\n\n$$\n50-9=41\n$$\n\nTherefore, Annie's total bill after the discount is $\\$41.$"] [17592186649876 :question/problem "Richard is a talent agent. For every booking for his clients, he gets a commission of $29\\%.$ If Richard \nbooks a client to appear on a television show for $\\$2,500,$ then how much does he earn in commission?"] [17592186649876 :question/worked-solution "Richard's commission is $29\\%$ of the price of the booking. In this case, the price of the booking is $\\$2,500.$ So, to calculate how much Richard earns in commission, we need to find $29\\%$ of $\\$2,500.$\n\nTo do this, we first write $29\\%$ as a decimal:\n\n$$\n29\\%=29÷100=0.29\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $2,500:$\n\n$$\n0.29\\times 2,500=725\n$$\n\nTherefore, Richard earns $\\$725$ in commission."] [17592186649881 :question/problem "$40\\%=$"] [17592186649881 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the number in front of the percent sign ($40$) as a fraction with denominator $100:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{40}{100}\n$$\n\nWe now need to simplify this fraction. Dividing the numerator and denominator by $10,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{40}{100}=\\frac{40÷10}{100÷10}=\\frac{4}{10}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{10}=\\frac{4÷2}{10÷2}=\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $40\\%=\\frac{2}{5}.$"] [17592186649882 :question/worked-solution "First, we write $\\frac{2}{5}$ as an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{2\\times 20}{5\\times 20}=\\frac{40}{100}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{40}{100}=40\\%\n$$"] [17592186649884 :question/problem "Freya's Footwear is having a sale in which a $5\\%$ markdown is applied to all pairs of shoes. If a pair of shoes cost $\\$40$ before the markdown is applied, how much is the markdown?"] [17592186649884 :question/worked-solution "There is a $5\\%$ markdown on the price of a pair of shoes. Here, the price of a pair of shoes is $\\$40.$ So to calculate the amount of the markdown, we need to find $5\\%$ of $\\$40.$\n\nTo do this, we first write $5\\%$ as a decimal:\n\n$$\n5\\%=5÷100=0.05\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $40:$\n\n$$\n0.05\\times 40=2\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of the markdown is $\\$2.$"] [17592186649885 :question/problem "Martha bought a sofa for $\\$550$ before tax. If the sales tax was $22\\%,$ then how much does she pay in tax?"] [17592186649885 :question/worked-solution "The tax rate is $22\\%$ of the price of the sofa. In this case, the price of the sofa is $\\$550.$ So, to calculate how much Martha pays in tax, we need to find $22\\%$ of $\\$550.$\n\nTo do this, we first write $22\\%$ as a decimal:\n\n$$\n22\\%=22÷100=0.22\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $550:$\n\n$$\n0.22\\times 550=121\n$$\n\nTherefore, Martha pays $\\$121$ in tax."] [17592186649886 :question/problem "Jeremy hires an artist to make a sculpture for $\\$1,700$ before the commission. If the commission was $22\\%,$ then what was Jeremy's total bill after commission."] [17592186649886 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the amount of commission that Jeremy must pay. Then, we will add that amount to the price of the sculpture.\n\nThe commission rate is $22\\%$ of the price of the sculpture, and the price of the sculpture is $\\$1,700.$ So, to calculate how much commission Jeremy must pay, we need to find $22\\%$ of $\\$1,700.$\n\nTo do this, we first write $22\\%$ as a decimal:\n\n$$\n22\\%=22÷100=0.22\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $1,700:$\n\n$$\n0.22\\times 1,700=374\n$$\n\nSo Jeremy must pay $\\$374$ in commission. We add this amount to the price of the sculpture:\n\n$$\n1,700+374=2,074\n$$\n\nTherefore, Jeremy's total bill after the commission is $\\$2,074.$"] [17592186649889 :question/problem "Dipak has a coupon in which a $24\\%$ discount is applied to all his groceries. If his total bill is $\\$125$ before the discount is applied, how much is the discount?"] [17592186649889 :question/worked-solution "There is a $24\\%$ discount on the price of the groceries. Here, the price of the groceries is $\\$125.$ So to calculate the amount of the discount, we need to find $24\\%$ of $\\$125.$\n\nTo do this, we first write $24\\%$ as a decimal:\n\n$$\n24\\%=24÷100=0.24\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $125:$\n\n$$\n0.24\\times 125=30\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of the discount is $\\$30.$"] [17592186649891 :question/problem "Flora's Flowers is having a sale in which a $10\\%$ markdown is applied to all items in the store. If Ivan buys a bunch of roses for $\\$30$ before the markdown is applied, what is Ivan's total bill after the markdown?"] [17592186649891 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the amount of the markdown. Then, we will subtract that amount from the price of the roses.\n\nThe markdown is $10\\%$ of the price of the roses, and the price of the roses is $\\$30.$ So, to calculate the amount of the markdown, we need to find $10\\%$ of $\\$30.$\n\nTo do this, we first write $10\\%$ as a decimal:\n\n$$\n10\\%=10÷100=0.1\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $30:$\n\n$$\n0.1\\times 30=3\n$$\n\nSo the amount of the markdown is $\\$3.$ We subtract this amount from the price of the roses:\n\n$$\n30-3=27\n$$\n\nTherefore, Ivan's total bill after the markdown is $\\$27.$"] [17592186649892 :question/problem "$210\\%=$"] [17592186649892 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the number in front of the percent sign ($210$) as a fraction with denominator $100:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{210}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $10:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{210}{100}=\\frac{210÷10}{100÷10}=\\frac{21}{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $210\\%=\\frac{21}{10}.$"] [17592186649894 :question/worked-solution "First, we write $\\frac{12}{25}$ as an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{25}=\\frac{12\\times 4}{25\\times 4}=\\frac{48}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{48}{100}=48\\%\n$$"] [17592186649898 :question/problem "This year, a cell phone company's sales are $110\\%$ of the sales in the previous year. What fraction is equivalent to this percentage?"] [17592186649898 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the number in front of the percent sign ($110$) as a fraction with denominator $100:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{110}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $10:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{110}{100}=\\frac{110÷10}{100÷10}=\\frac{11}{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $110\\%=\\frac{11}{10}.$"] [17592186649900 :question/problem "Antony wishes to sell his books for $\\$10$ before the publisher's commission. If the commission was $25\\%,$ then what is the retail price for the book after commission?"] [17592186649900 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the amount of commission for each book. Then, we will add that amount to the price of a book.\n\nThe commission rate is $25\\%$ of the price of the book, and the price of the book is $\\$10.$ So, to calculate how much commission buyers will pay, we need to find $25\\%$ of $\\$10.$\n\nTo do this, we first write $25\\%$ as a decimal:\n\n$$\n25\\%=25÷100=0.25\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $10:$\n\n$$\n0.25\\times 10=2.5\n$$\n\nSo buyers must pay $\\$2.50$ in commission. We add this amount to the price of the book:\n\n$$\n10+2.5=12.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the retail price after the commission is $\\$12.50.$"] [17592186649901 :question/problem "Kate is a real estate agent. For every house she sells, she gets a commission of $2\\%.$ If Kate sells a house for $\\$60,000,$ then how much does she earn in commission?"]}
